data: {"seq":33,"ts":"2026-08-23T08:39:24.805Z","text":"=stdin, binary_md5 of that ELF, payload or payload_hex (short).\n3. Poll fw_get_trace until not running. Record trace_id, status, request.via, trigger_result.kind, diff.function_count.\n4. Do another trace with input_path=/tmp/poc.bin + payload_hex, poll and record. Empty diff must be reported honestly. No record_finding unless stdin diff really hits a dangerous function.\n\nGood candidates for stdin/file readers: busybox (multicall applets like gunzip, tar, md5sum read stdin/files), minidlnad... Let me pick a simple, deterministic one. busybox with argv0 applet e.g. \"md5sum\" or \"cat\"? Actually for a real parser, busybox \"gunzip\" reads stdin (gz stream). Or use `tail`/`head`. A file parser: busybox `unzip`? On MIPS qemu-user, busybox should run fine.\n\nLet me pick busybox (md5 aa3d98e20b0f5904d08b51eed6a9536a) with argv0 like \"md5sum\" and feed stdin. Or better, use a decompression applet that buffers input heavily. For capability validation, md5sum is fine — diff will show parsing of stdin.\n\nActually, for something more interesting, there's `bin/minidlnad` which parses media files (via libavformat). But feeding it stdin as a media file... minidlnad needs config. Keep it simple.\n\nLet me use busybox applet \"md5sum\": argv0=md5sum, payload=\"hello\" via stdin. That reads stdin. Diff should show hashing functions in busybox given the input vs empty.\n\nActually baseline vs trigger: baseline is empty stdin, trigger is \"hello\". md5sum will run either way, but hashing differs — function coverage may be identical (same code path). Empty diff might be the honest result for md5sum. Better to pick something where input changes control flow: e.g. busybox \"gunzip\" with invalid gzip header vs empty (invalid header too)... Hmm.\n\nChoose busybox applet that parses structured input with branching: \"ar\"? \"tar\" list? Or \"uncompress\"? Let's choose argv0=\"tar\" with payload as a tar header? Or simpler: \"head -c\"? \n\nBetter: busybox \"gzip -d\" reading stdin. Payload \"hello\" -> gunzip error path, empty stdin -> also error but different? Not sure.\n\nActually a well-known branching input: busybox `watch`, `cryptpw`... Let me not overthink. The point is capability: get a stdin trace and an input_path trace, record results honestly including empty diffs.\n\nLet me pick busybox with argv0 \"md5sum\" for trace 1 (stdin): payload \"hello\". For trace 2 (input_path=/tmp/poc.bin): specify input_path, and also give stdin? No — input_path drops payload as file, and argv could reference it. For busybox md5sum, argv [\"/tmp/poc.bin\"] reading file bytes written from payload_hex. Wait: does input_path take the file content from payload/payload_hex or stdin? \"input_path=/tmp/<name> to drop payload as a file (file parsers)\". So the payload bytes get dropped as the file; for content I think we use payload or payload_hex (probably the same bytes as payload). Let me pass payload_hex = hex of \"hello\" and argv [\"/tmp/poc.bin\"], argv0=\"md5sum\".\n\nHmm, but does input_path require via=stdin or can it be anywhere? \"input_path=/tmp/<name> drops the bytes as a guest file.\" For file parsers. So trace 2: binary_md5=busybox, input_path=/tmp/poc.bin, payload_hex=68656c6c6f (hex of \"hello\"), argv=[\"/tmp/poc.bin\"], argv0=\"md5sum\". via? Not net, so stdin default? via=stdin is the explicit option. Since no port, default is stdin. That's fine.\n\nAlso sanity check: does busybox md5sum with argv0 work under qemu-user? calling main with argv[0]=md5sum selects applet. trace argv0 field \"Forge guest argv[0] for busybox multicall\" — yes.\n\nLet me also think about a file-parser ELF that's not busybox and deterministic: minidlnad parses media but needs sqlite db. skip","stream":true,"block":0}

