event: thinking
data: {"seq":85,"ts":"2026-08-23T08:39:46.360Z","text":"Trace 1 failed: `QemuError: empty coverage log (target rc=2); sh: 1: cannot open /tmp/fwgraph-stdin: No such file`. So the stdin via mechanism dropped the file at /tmp/fwgraph-stdin, but busybox sh on this firmware — when the applet is \"sh\" invoked non-interactively without a script or -c, ash reads from stdin which was redirected to /tmp/fwgraph-stdin, but the file doesn't exist? Interesting: the harness writes stdin to /tmp/fwgraph-stdin inside the guest and redirects fd0. But the error says \"cannot open /tmp/fwgraph-stdin: No such file\" — hmm, that's the shell reporting an error when opening... Actually ash applet with no argv: when stdin is not a tty, ash reads commands from stdin. The message suggests the harness ran busybox sh with stdin redirected from a file that was never created, or the file path is seen differently.\n\nThis looks like a platform quirk: maybe stdin via file-drop requires the file to be created by the trace runner, and it wasn't. But the error says target rc=2, empty coverage.\n\nOption: use argv with -c \"echo hello\" so sh doesn't try to read the stdin file at all, but then payload isn't used. However payload_bytes still 10, via stdin. If I give argv [\"-c\",\"echo hello\"] and payload \"echo hello\", then sh runs the command from -c, ignoring stdin. Coverage would come from busybox main → ash. Diff vs baseline (empty) — baseline: same argv [\"-c\",\"echo hello\"]? Baseline always runs with same argv and no payload. Hmm, if baseline is argv [\"-c\",\"\"] then diff vs \"echo hello\" could show parsing differences.\n\nActually simpler and more robust: use an applet that reads stdin directly without the \"cannot open file\" issue — e.g. `md5sum` reads stdin fd0. If the harness redirects stdin from /tmp/fwgraph-stdin and the file exists, md5sum would read the 10 bytes. The earlier failure was \"cannot open /tmp/fwgraph-stdin: No such file\" — that's the ash builtin `read`/redirection or ash","stream":true,"block":0}

event: thinking
data: {"seq":86,"ts":"2026-08-23T08:39:46.711Z","text":"Trace 1 failed: `QemuError: empty coverage log (target rc=2); sh: 1: cannot open /tmp/fwgraph-stdin: No such file`. So the stdin via mechanism dropped the file at /tmp/fwgraph-stdin, but busybox sh on this firmware — when the applet is \"sh\" invoked non-interactively without a script or -c, ash reads from stdin which was redirected to /tmp/fwgraph-stdin, but the file doesn't exist? Interesting: the harness writes stdin to /tmp/fwgraph-stdin inside the guest and redirects fd0. But the error says \"cannot open /tmp/fwgraph-stdin: No such file\" — hmm, that's the shell reporting an error when opening... Actually ash applet with no argv: when stdin is not a tty, ash reads commands from stdin. The message suggests the harness ran busybox sh with stdin redirected from a file that was never created, or the file path is seen differently.\n\nThis looks like a platform quirk: maybe stdin via file-drop requires the file to be created by the trace runner, and it wasn't. But the error says target rc=2, empty coverage.\n\nOption: use argv with -c \"echo hello\" so sh doesn't try to read the stdin file at all, but then payload isn't used. However payload_bytes still 10, via stdin. If I give argv [\"-c\",\"echo hello\"] and payload \"echo hello\", then sh runs the command from -c, ignoring stdin. Coverage would come from busybox main → ash. Diff vs baseline (empty) — baseline: same argv [\"-c\",\"echo hello\"]? Baseline always runs with same argv and no payload. Hmm, if baseline is argv [\"-c\",\"\"] then diff vs \"echo hello\" could show parsing differences.\n\nActually simpler and more robust: use an applet that reads stdin directly without the \"cannot open file\" issue — e.g. `md5sum` reads stdin fd0. If the harness redirects stdin from /tmp/fwgraph-stdin and the file exists, md5sum would read the 10 bytes. The earlier failure was \"cannot open /tmp/fwgraph-stdin: No such file\" — that's the ash builtin `read`/redirection or ash initializing? Actually the message format `sh: 1: cannot open X` — busybox ash prints `sh: %d: cannot open %s` when a redirection fails. So ash tries to open /tmp/fwgraph-stdin (its interactive command source is fd0, but maybe the harness implements stdin by passing `-c` ... no).\n\nWait — maybe the harness runs qemu with stdin redirected to /tmp/fwgraph-stdin guest file, and busybox applet \"sh\" tries `open(\"/tmp/fwgraph-stdin\")`? No, ash reads fd 0 directly.\n\nHmm, actually busybox ash wrappers: when invoked as \"sh\" without -c, if stdin is not a tty, it reads from fd0. The error might come from ash trying to open the script path... not applicable.\n\nAlternatively the harness might implement stdin via `-c '...'`? No.\n\n","stream":true,"block":0}

