data: {"seq":95,"ts":"2026-08-23T08:39:50.262Z","text":"Trace 1 failed: `QemuError: empty coverage log (target rc=2); sh: 1: cannot open /tmp/fwgraph-stdin: No such file`. So the stdin via mechanism dropped the file at /tmp/fwgraph-stdin, but busybox sh on this firmware — when the applet is \"sh\" invoked non-interactively without a script or -c, ash reads from stdin which was redirected to /tmp/fwgraph-stdin, but the file doesn't exist? Interesting: the harness writes stdin to /tmp/fwgraph-stdin inside the guest and redirects fd0. But the error says \"cannot open /tmp/fwgraph-stdin: No such file\" — hmm, that's the shell reporting an error when opening... Actually ash applet with no argv: when stdin is not a tty, ash reads commands from stdin. The message suggests the harness ran busybox sh with stdin redirected from a file that was never created, or the file path is seen differently.\n\nThis looks like a platform quirk: maybe stdin via file-drop requires the file to be created by the trace runner, and it wasn't. But the error says target rc=2, empty coverage.\n\nOption: use argv with -c \"echo hello\" so sh doesn't try to read the stdin file at all, but then payload isn't used. However payload_bytes still 10, via stdin. If I give argv [\"-c\",\"echo hello\"] and payload \"echo hello\", then sh runs the command from -c, ignoring stdin. Coverage would come from busybox main → ash. Diff vs baseline (empty) — baseline: same argv [\"-c\",\"echo hello\"]? Baseline always runs with same argv and no payload. Hmm, if baseline is argv [\"-c\",\"\"] then diff vs \"echo hello\" could show parsing differences.\n\nActually simpler and more robust: use an applet that reads stdin directly without the \"cannot open file\" issue — e.g. `md5sum` reads stdin fd0. If the harness redirects stdin from /tmp/fwgraph-stdin and the file exists, md5sum would read the 10 bytes. The earlier failure was \"cannot open /tmp/fwgraph-stdin: No such file\" — that's the ash builtin `read`/redirection or ash initializing? Actually the message format `sh: 1: cannot open X` — busybox ash prints `sh: %d: cannot open %s` when a redirection fails. So ash tries to open /tmp/fwgraph-stdin (its interactive command source is fd0, but maybe the harness implements stdin by passing `-c` ... no).\n\nWait — maybe the harness runs qemu with stdin redirected to /tmp/fwgraph-stdin guest file, and busybox applet \"sh\" tries `open(\"/tmp/fwgraph-stdin\")`? No, ash reads fd 0 directly.\n\nHmm, actually busybox ash wrappers: when invoked as \"sh\" without -c, if stdin is not a tty, it reads from fd0. The error might come from ash trying to open the script path... not applicable.\n\nAlternatively the harness might implement stdin via `-c '...'`? No.\n\nLet me just retry with a different, simpler applet: md5sum. argv0=\"md5sum\", no argv, payload \"hello\" via stdin. If the harness writes stdin to /tmp/fwgraph-stdin and md5sum opens fd0 directly... md5sum reads fd 0 (not by path), so it won't try to open any file. The earlier error was sh-specific (ash tries to open the file for stdin? weird but anyway).\n\nAlso possible: the stdin file is created at /tmp/fwgraph-stdin but 'sh' without -c requires it — anyway, md5sum avoids path open.\n\nFor trace 2's failure root cause it's the same issue (sh can't open stdin file). So both traces failed for the same reason.\n\nRetry plan:\n- Trace 1 (stdin): busybox md5sum, argv0=\"md5sum\", payload=\"hello\" (or a longish unique string), via=stdin.\n- Trace 2 (file): busybox md5sum? No — input_path=/tmp/poc.bin then argv=[\"/tmp/poc.bin\"] and md5sum opens the file by path — but that's a file parser that opens by path not stdin; and payload_hex drops the bytes as the file. That works: `md5sum /tmp/poc.bin` reads the file. But then also stdin feed: payload_bytes will also be fed to stdin? The request shows payload_steps:1 with via stdin — harness may also write it to stdin. md5sum with a file arg ignores stdin. Fine.\n\nBut honestly for a \"读文件的 ELF\" I'd prefer something like busybox `unzip -l /tmp/poc.bin` (structured file parser) or `tar -t -f`. A zip parser: busybox unzip with a small crafted zip would give interesting coverage diff. But crafting zip hex is more work;to keep capability acceptance simple, md5sum file read is fine, and I can report both honestly. Actually let me pick something with actual parsing to make the diff meaningful: `busybox unzip -l /tmp/poc.bin` needs valid zip. Too much.\n\nAlternative richer stdin parser: busybox applet `base64`? decodes stdin... `base64` vs `base64 -d`. Parsing base64 input \"aGVsbG8=\" — base64 decode from stdin. Function-wise, decode path chosen.","stream":true,"block":0}

