o be non-empty AND the harness writes it — maybe harness writes stdin payload only when provide `payload` (string), and I did provide payload. And it still says file missing.\n\nAlternatively use a non-busybox ELF that reads stdin by itself, like `dropbearkey`? It reads key files. `vsftpd` reads config file + stdin? Actually vsftpd is a daemon; reading stdin isn't natural but vsftpd can run with `-olocal_umask=...`? Meh.\n\nWhat about `ntfs-3g`? Reads block device... needs args.\n\n`minidlnad` file parsers: it parses media files (libavformat/libexif) — that's a real file-reader attack surface. But running minidlnad requires config/db; probably exits immediately. Maybe `minidlnad -f /tmp/poc.bin`? Unknown CLI.\n\nSimplest robust file-input ELF: busybox with input_path. c30ceb41dcef (busybox md5sum argv=[\"/tmp/poc.bin\"], input_path=/tmp/poc.bin) is still running — but given the stdin-file weirdness it may fail the same way IF via=stdin always appends sh script. Wait, but this one has argv=[\"/tmp/poc.bin\"], and if harness builds `busybox md5sum /tmp/poc.bin`... but argv0 ignored → `busybox sh /tmp/fwgraph-stdin` still? Let me poll it; maybe with input_path present, the harness does something different (runs argv as given, feeds stdin file too).\n\nActually I bet the harness command construction is:\n`qemu-mips -0 <argv0> busybox <argv> < via file>`? and if argv0 ignored → busybox (basename \"busybox\")... prints usage? but error is \"sh\".\n\nOK let me just poll c","stream":true,"block":0}

