data: {"seq":166,"ts":"2026-08-23T08:40:21.913Z","text":"Interesting: trace eb2d054e452a failed with the SAME error: `sh: 1: cannot open /tmp/fwgraph-stdin: No such file` even with argv0=md5sum! That means the harness is NOT honoring argv0 as busybox applet selection — or the harness itself runs the binary with some wrapper that uses `sh`? Wait, the error says \"sh: 1: cannot open ...\" — busybox applet named in the error is \"sh\". So the binary's argv0 was \"sh\", not \"md5sum\"?\n\nHmm. Perhaps the harness's fork/exec of busybox passes argv0 as path basename? No wait — maybe the harness builds the qemu command as: qemu-mips /path/to/busybox <args>, and the argv0 override is implemented as a symlink or via `busybox sh`? Actually the earlier trace d43b56e1edd1 had argv0=\"sh\" and errored with \"sh: 1: cannot open\". This trace eb2d054e452a argv0=\"md5sum\" but the error still says \"sh: 1:\"! So the argv0 I set was IGNORED, and it ran as \"sh\" anyway. Or maybe the harness ignores argv0 entirely and runs `/bin/busybox` with basename... but basename is \"busybox\" which with no applet args prints usage normally (not sh).\n\nWait — maybe the harness implements argv0 by prepending \"argv0 \" to the command: qemu-mips busybox sh? No, argv was empty.\n\nHmm, \"sh: 1: cannot open /tmp/fwgraph-stdin\" — busybox ash prints this when it tries to open its script file. Where does /tmp/fwgraph-stdin come from? The harness, for via=stdin, presumably executes: `qemu-mips busybox sh /tmp/fwgraph-stdin`? That would explain: it runs applet \"sh\" with script path /tmp/fwgraph-stdin, and that file doesn't exist in the guest. So the harness hardcodes applet selection to \"sh <stdin-file>\" regardless of argv0?? That seems odd.\n\nOr maybe: the harness runs the binary normally with argv0 from binary path basename... no.\n\nAlternatively the harness might copy stdin bytes to guest file /tmp/fwgraph-stdin and run: `qemu -0 sh ... /bin/busybox sh /tmp/fwgraph-stdin`? The file missing might be because input_path drop failed or the file write step is broken for pure-stdin payloads (payload without input_path). Notice: in c30ceb41dcef (which has input_path=/tmp/poc.bin AND payload_hex), still running — maybe THAT one works because with input_path set, the file-drop works and the stdin redirection also works?\n\nBut the error for eb2d054e452a says cannot open /tmp/fwgraph-stdin — that's the stdin file, not /tmp/poc.bin.\n\nHmm wait, actually maybe the flow is: via=stdin → harness writes payload to guest /tmp/fwgraph-stdin (must exist) and invokes busybox as `sh` reading that file? But then argv0 should have been honored... The error would be from qemu prefix like `-0 md5sum`? No, ash error prefix is the applet name \"sh\".\n\nLet me reconsider: maybe the harness ignores argv0 entirely and always appends /tmp/fwgraph-stdin as a script when via=stdin, invoking `busybox sh /tmp/fwgraph-stdin`? Then applet is sh regardless. That matches: error \"sh: 1: cannot open /tmp/fwgraph-stdin\". So for via=stdin, the harness runs the busybox with arg [sh, /tmp/fwgraph-stdin]? But then argv field we set would conflict... c30ceb41dcef has argv=[\"/tmp/poc.bin\"] and is running — maybe argv IS used and it runs `busybox md5sum /tmp/poc.bin`? or `busybox sh /tmp/poc.bin`? and stdin file ALSO missing → same error expected for c30ceb41dcef.\n\nHmm wait, but in trace d43b56e1edd1 argv was [], argv0 sh — error mentions /tmp/fwgraph-stdin. In eb2d054e452a argv [] argv0 md5sum — same error. So indeed argv0 is ignored for the error prefix but the \"1:\" argument position suggests `sh: 1: cannot open` means busybox applet=\"sh\" failed at its script open, \"1\" being... hmm in busybox ash, the \"1:\" is not a count; format is \"applet: %s: cannot open\" where %s might be the redirection target... Actually the stderr format for ash redirection errors is like `sh: 1: cannot open /path`: the \"1\" might be a line number? For non-interactive reading from a script... could be line number 1? No.\n\nWhatever. The key insight: with via=stdin, the harness executes busybox with applet \"sh\" and a stdin file path /tmp/fwgraph-stdin, and that drop failed → rc=2. This is a platform quirk: busybox + via=stdin combo fails because the stdin-file drop doesn't happen for pure payload (no input_path). \n\nAlternative approach: try via=net? No — user said avoid network ports, and for a stdin/file ELF. Actually via=net requires port.\n\nBetter: use the stdin file path that the harness expects: maybe if I set input_path=/tmp/fwgraph-stdin? No — input_path is for dropping payload as a named file; the harness itself uses /tmp/fwgraph-stdin for stdin payload. Since the error shows the harness DID try to reference /tmp/fwgraph-stdin as sh's script (meaning the harness ran `busybox sh /tmp/fwgraph-stdin`), the actual bug is the file not being created. Maybe it's created but ash can't open it because... the harness chdirs or uses a different root? Hmm.\n\nAlternative: skip busybox. Pick a real standalone ELF that reads stdin and is not a network daemon (avoid :80/:445 per user). Standalone non-daemon stdin readers in this firmware: `dropbearkey` (reads stdin for passphrase? It's interactive on tty), `smbpasswd` (reads stdin: prompts for password via getpass), vsftpd reads config... Actually for file-input models: minidlnad reads media files (libavformat), but it needs DB/config.\n\nWhat about `fw_qemu_exec`? That's a one-shot crash-check tool that accepts stdin — user asked specifically for fw_request_trace/fw_get_trace capability validation though. But we could use fw_qemu_exec to validate the binary itself runs (different tool, budget 8). The task is: demonstrate trace capability with stdin diff and input_path diff. The trace tool seems to have a quirk with via=stdin + busybox.\n\nKey experiment: does via=stdin work with input_path set? c30ceb41dcef has BOTH input_path=/tmp/poc.bin and payload_hex, via stdin, argv [\"/tmp/poc.bin\"]. If it fails with the same \"c