data: {"seq":204,"ts":"2026-08-23T08:40:37.847Z","text":"Very informative!\n\n1. `fw_qemu_exec` (qe-4cdc7232): busybox with argv0 md5sum printed the **usage screen** — meaning **argv0 was ignored in fw_qemu_exec too** — the binary ran as basename \"busybox\" with empty argv → usage. result \"ok\", returncode 0. So `argv0` forging is NOT applied by the qemu infrastructure in this platform for busybox. That's why trace attempts ran as `sh`?? Wait no, usage printed means it ran as \"busybox\" not \"sh\". But the trace error said \"sh: 1: cannot open /tmp/fwgraph-stdin\".\n\nHmm wait — the usage list shows \"sh\" and \"msh\" are applets. Where does \"sh: 1: cannot open\" come from then? Maybe the trace harness DOES pass argv0 differently: via qemu `-0` flag? qemu-user supports `-0 argv0` on some versions... The fw_qemu_exec output didn't honor argv0 (usage printed). But maybe in fw_request_trace, the harness uses a different launch that DOES honor argv0? Contradiction: trace with argv0=md5sum reported error prefix \"sh\".\n\nUnless... the trace harness constructs `busybox sh /tmp/fwgraph-stdin` when argv is empty regardless: i.e., it forces applet \"sh\" for via=stdin. And for argv0=md5sum it... hmm still sh.\n\n2. `c30ceb41dcef` SUCCEEDED: status `ok_empty_diff`! With request.via = **\"file\"** (the harness reclassified via=stdin→file because input_path was set). trigger_result.kind = **\"input_path\"**, path /tmp/poc.bin, sent 5. diff.function_count = **0** (empty diff). Baseline functions 26, trigger functions 26, tb 960 both. So the input_file path works: payload dropped as /tmp/poc.bin, argv=[\"/tmp/poc.bin\"] presumably ran as `busybox md5sum /tmp/poc.bin`? Actually given argv0 seems ignored, argv=[\"/tmp/poc.bin\"] appended to busybox → `busybox /tmp/poc.bin` → but busybox runs the first arg as applet name! `/tmp/poc.bin` isn't an applet → usage... but we got 26 functions and rc OK, so something ran. Actually maybe the harness DOES honor argv0 here: `md5sum /tmp/poc.bin`. functions 26. Fine.\n\nSo: file-input trace via input_path WORKS (ok_empty_diff). The pure-stdin via=stdin path for busybox is broken by the harness: it creates /tmp/fwgraph-stdin inside... something, and sh can't open it. Actually wait — re-read the failure: `sh: 1: cannot open /tmp/fwgraph-stdin: No such file`. Since argv0 was ignored in qemu (proven by usage screen), the trace harness probably constructs `busybox sh /tmp/fwgraph-stdin`? Where would \"sh\" come from... Maybe the harness, for via=stdin with argv0 set, runs `qemu -0 <argv0> binary <argv>` and appends `/tmp/fwgraph-stdin` as an argument, and since argv0 IS honored by qemu -0 (unlike fw_qemu_exec), argv0=md5sum would have arg `/tmp/fwgraph-stdin` → md5sum: /tmp/fwgraph-stdin: No such file... but the error says \"sh: 1:\". Ugh.\n\nWhatever the exact mechanism, the empirical facts:\n- via=stdin + busybox → fails with `cannot open /tmp/fwgraph-stdin` (file drop missing in that path).\n- via=input_path (file) → works: trace c30ceb41dcef = ok_empty_diff, trigger_result.kind=input_path, diff.function_count=0.\n\nDoes pure stdin differencing work AT ALL with a different binary? The failure was busybox-specific-ish (path open). Try a real ELF that reads fd0 directly and is standalone. Requirement: \"会读 stdin/文件的 ELF（不要再打 :80/:445 网络口）\"。 \n\nCandidates that read stdin:\n- dropbearkey: interactive passphrase... reads from tty not stdin.\n- busybox applets: cat, tftp (client), logger (reads stdin? logger reads messages pipe), getty no.\n- `smbpasswd` reads tty.\n- `vsftpd`: reads config file (file-input attacker-controlled? config is local), or commands? No.\n\nSince argv0 forging is broken on this platform, busybox multicall needs argv to select applet: `argv=[\"sh\"]` etc. Let me try via=stdin with argv=[\"sh\",\"/tmp/fwgraph-stdin\"]? Hmm the harness itself injects /tmp/fwgraph-stdin as the stdin source...\n\nAlternative: qemu-user stdin: the harness presumably sets up stdin via a file since qemu-user cant' easily pipe? Actually qemu-user CAN pipe host stdin directly. The traces failing suggest the harness uses a guest-side file for stdin (dropped from host), and that drop is broken for pure-stdin trace runs (no input_path). Possibly a platform bug: pure via=stdin payload isn't materialized. But user says don't claim platform capability missing — but we should report honestly what happened. Note: the user said \"禁止说缺少平台能力\" — forbid claiming missing platform capabilities as an excuse without evidence. But here we have concrete trace evidence: file-input path works, pure-stdin path errors with a real harness error (rc=2). That's factual reporting, not excuse-making — the user explicitly said \"空差分也要如实写\" i.e., honest reporting.\n\nLet me try ONE more pure-stdin variant with a non-busybox binary to see if pure stdin works at all. Which ELF reads stdin? A standalone one that parses: hmm. In this firmware, candidates: \n- `usr/bin/dropbearkey`? no.\n- `bin/arpping`? no.\n- `usr/sbin/dhcp6c`? network.\n- `ntfs-3g`? device.\n- What about a binary that by default reads a FILE (config) — that's the input_path case which already works.\n\nHonestly the c30ceb41dcef trace already satisfies step 4 (input_path=/tmp/poc.bin + payload_hex → ok_empty_diff). For step 3 (via=stdin pure), I have two failed attempts with trace_id+status recorded. To be thorough, let me try one more: via=stdin with busybox but argv=[\"sh\"] (selecting applet via argv instead of argv0) — maybe with applet \"sh\" the harness's /tmp/fwgraph-stdin IS created... it wasn't. Or try `cat`: argv=[\"cat\"]? busybox cat with stdin file missing → \"cat: /tmp/fwgraph-stdin: No such file\" likely.\n\nHmm, waste. Better: try via=stdin on a REAL parser binary that reads fd0 itself. Look at the binary list for something like a config parser / image parser used as CLI. We have `bin/isgnsta` (config?), `usr/sbin/...`. Search for binaries reading stdin via fw_search? fw_search searches functions; let me search functions like \"read\" from stdin? Too broad.\n\nAlternative: use `tftp` applet client loading a file. Meh.\n\nActually — maybe try via=stdin with `argv=[\"","stream":true,"block":0}

