data: {"seq":16,"ts":"2026-08-23T09:07:11.543Z","text":"The user says \"继续挖掘\" (continue mining). Earlier context: the user said only do capability acceptance, don't mine new vulns, don't record_finding. Now they say \"继续挖掘\" — continue mining. The initial system prompt is a firmware vulnerability mining agent. So now the user wants actual vulnerability mining to resume.\n\nLet me recap what we know:\n- Job: 3a1f3c822c22, Archer C7 v2 firmware (TP-Link ArcherC7v2_en_us_3_15_4_up(260427).bin)\n- MIPS 32-bit BE\n- Binary of interest: busybox (aa3d98e20b0f5904d08b51eed6a9536a)\n- The stdin trace works now: f55640905b63 (md5sum, empty diff)\n- input_path trace works: a432876827e2 (sh, diff 45), c30ceb41dcef (md5sum file, empty diff)\n\nNow for real mining:\n1. Check surfaces / identification inputs (I have IN-001..IN-015+; there were 15 inputs, 20 candidates).\n2. Attack surface docs (fw_list_surfaces) - get routing, dispatchers, parsers.\n3. Find suspicious functions with fw_get_function_source (kind=brief first).\n4. Differential traces via stdin/file to hit parser functions.\n5. Fuzz if needed (fw_request_fuzz).\n6. Record findings when real (with trace_id for observed/verified).\n\nGiven the priority: the stdin/file input path just got validated. Which binaries read stdin/files that could be vulnerable parsers in this firmware?\n\nCandidates from binaries list:\n- busybox (aa3d98e20b0f5904d08b51eed6a9536a) — has applets: arping, brctl, cat, chmod, date, df, echo, ethreg, false, getty, ifconfig, init, insmod, kill, klogd, linuxrc, ln, logger, login, logread, ls, lsmod, mount, msh, ping, ps, reboot, rm, rmmod, route, sh, sleep, syslogd, test, tftp, top, true, udhcpc, udhcpd, umount, vconfig\n  - Note: this is BusyBox v1.01 — old, from 2026 build date but version 1.01 is ancient. Old busybox versions have known vulnerabilities (e.g., CVE-2006-1056? etc.), but we should find concrete ones in specific applets: tftp (client), udhcpc/udhcpd (network parsing), syslogd, getty, login...\n  - udhcpc/udhcpd parse network packets — network interfaces though.\n  - tftp client reads files/network.\n  \n- samba_multicall / smbpasswd (8de9773f9eb69f9ed433c6a50d053ea2) — smbpasswd reads stdin? It prompts for passwords; could be a stdin parser (getpass reads from /dev/tty or stdin fallback).\n- dropbear (831411ed7e1770609b0ab9837d576e2f) — SSH, network port 22. Already identified IN-013. Dropbear KEX parsing is a classic target.\n- httpd (9582aa23b0bc2de099b9997b09457d01) — port 80. But user earlier said don't hit :80/:445 network ports in the previous turn; now \"继续挖掘\" may lift restrictions. But we have stdin/file trace capability; for httpd we'd use net via port 80 which is allowed by tools (fw_request_trace with port). Actually for httpd, better to use fw_routes / fw_attack_surface to find handlers, then static analysis of parsers, then differential traces via stdin with request_path? For HTTP, via=net with port 80. The earlier instruction \"不要再打 :80/:445 网络口\" was scoped to the capability acceptance turn. Now mining resumes.\n\nLet me start by checking the attack surface and surfaces list to get the routing/parser picture, and also list traces to see what's been run.\n\nStrategy: Use fw_list_surfaces + fw_get_identification (already have) + fw_attack_surface to get scored source→sink paths. And fw_list_traces to see baseline.\n\nLet me fire parallel:\n- fw_list_surfaces\n- fw_attack_surface (brief)\n- fw_list_traces\n\nThen dig into the most promising paths.","stream":true,"block":0}

