data: {"seq":52,"ts":"2026-08-23T09:07:25.072Z","text":"The system has 432 traces already (mostly ok_empty_diff). The attack surface shows 20 P0/P1 candidates. Let me look at the identification's network inputs and surfaces more carefully.\n\nKey candidates from attack_surface:\n1. **Path 165af590169e4972 (P0)**: binary 23e3796091277a8be1e69dc8f5093dbd (dhcp6s — IN-004, IPv6 DHCP server on TCP? actually dhcp6s is DHCPv6 server, UDP 547 normally; identification says TCP though), main 0x407ff8 → sub_40F0F8 (0x40f0f8) which does vsprintf(s, format, arg) then system(s). CWE-78 cmdi. main has getaddrinfo + memcpy too.\n2. **Path 13ad3af11ed2d97b (P0)**: binary 08b0860102c4b034bfbae2238115a7e6, network_thread 0x4114d0 line 287 strcpy to (char*)(j+916), v10 from network. CWE-121 bof.\n3. **Path 2029048cc44a3235 (P0)**: binary 2c508f48e714f8276fee71652f19b4ba, main 0x400e28 multiple strcpy to stack vars from argv/network. CWE-121.\n\nBinaries referenced:\n- 23e3796091277a8be1e69dc8f5093dbd = dhcp6s (usr/sbin/dhcp6s) — IN-004\n- 08b0860102c4b034bfbae2238115a7e6 = ? not in identification listing I saw... need fw_list_binaries? It didn't appear in the first page maybe. Let me check via identification: bpalogin? No that's f8a1d1f6. Hmm 08b086... not seen. Search binaries.\n- 2c508f48e714f8276fee71652f19b4ba = ? also unknown.\n\nThe user said \"不要挖新洞\" earlier but now says \"继续挖掘\" — so mining resumes. The user's ops note in this turn: \"只做能力验收\" was last turn; this turn says 继续挖掘. So I'm free to mine and record_finding (real ones).\n\nPlan: dig the P0 paths.\n1. dhcp6s: main 0x407ff8 → sub_40F0F8 vsprintf+system. dhcp6s is a DHCPv6 server — it processes client packets (network input). But identification says dhcp6s is \"tcp\"?! Weird. dhcp6s normally UDP 547. Anyway, its input parser can be traced via stdin (feed raw DHCPv6 packet)? The parser reads from network socket though — via stdin won't hit recvfrom path... but dhcp6s might have config file parsing too.\n\nActually a more practical angle: these network daemons can be exercised with fw_request_trace via=net with port, since we know their ports? dhcp6s port 547 UDP. But trace tool is TCP-based (\"net sends HTTP GET or payload...\"). Hmm.\n\nAlternative dynamic approach per field guidance: use via=stdin to feed parser functions that read from fd, or QEMU whole-binary.\n\nActually, for dhcp6s, the parser for DHCPv6 messages is likely a function that takes (buf, len) - we could fuzz it with fw_request_fuzz whole-binary or function-targeted.\n\nLet me be systematic. First read the surfaces AS-004 (dhcp6s) and check binaries for 08b08601 and 2c508f48.\n\nActually 08b0860102c4b034bfbae2238115a7e6 and 2c508f48e714f8276fee71652f19b4ba are probably other daemons (maybe ipcserver? uclited? atead? ). Let me search fw_list_binaries gave 133 binaries but truncated at 100000... I only saw up to \"0098fba848\". Let me get the full list? fw_list_binaries returns full manifest; it got truncated in display. Better: use fw_search or check surfaces files.\n\nLet me fetch surfaces: AS-004 (dhcp6s), and look at identification full for candidate list with md5s (I have IN-001..IN-015). IN-014 httpd 9582aa23, IN-013 dropbear 831411ed, IN-012 vsftpd 78023622, IN-011 uclited d61e67ed, IN-010 samba 8de9773f, IN-009 scp / IN-006 dropbearkey / IN-013 dropbear share 831411ed, IN-008 minidlnad 3e3258c9, IN-007 ipcserver 0b3a37ef, IN-005 dnsproxy 5bf87d07, IN-003 dhcp6c ae35a53c, IN-004 dhcp6s 23e37960, IN-001 athadhoc 53a5015a, IN-002 bpalogin f8a1d1f6.\n\nSo 08b0860102c4b034bfbae2238115a7e6 and 2c508f48e714f8276fee71652f19b4ba are NOT in the identification inputs list visible — could be busybox? busybox is aa3d98e2. 2c508f48 could be minidlnad-related? minidlnad is 3e3258c9. Hmm. Wait — maybe 08b0860102c4... is `usr/sbin/pppd` or `xl2tpd` or `bpalogin`? Let me identify these md5s by searching the binaries list. The fw_list_binaries output was truncated mid-list. Let me instead use fw_get_surface AS-014 or whatever corresponds, and/or fw_search won't help md5.\n\nI can use fw_browse_firmware? No md5 there. Best: call fw_list_binaries again? It'll show same truncation. Alternatively I can run fw_get_surface for each AS file to see which binary they reference. Let me fetch several surfaces: AS-001..AS-015 mapping, but that's 15 calls. Maybe fetch the identification full? That'd show candidate md5s. Let me try identification full=true (large). Hmm.\n\nDo targeted: fw_get_surface for AS-004 (dhcp6s) to see parser/dispatcher functions → that's the P0 cmdi path. Also fetch AS-003? Let me first map AS files to inputs. The listing order: surfaces 15, but files list only names. The per-input list (fw_list_surfaces) would give AS-xxx per input with routing etc. I fetched that? fw_list_surfaces returned only the summary + filenames (no per-input detail in this version apparently). Hmm, actually the response included \"files\" but not mapping of inputs.\n\nLet me just fetch the identification with full=true to get dispatch chains with md5s, and in parallel fetch function source for dhcp6s main (0x407ff8) and sub_40F0F8 (0x40f0f8) from 23e3796091277a8be1e69dc8f5093dbd.\n\nActually the user wants mining to continue — quickest high-value: dhcp6s cmdi path. Let me read:\n- fw_get_function_source md5=23e3796091277a8be1e69dc8f5093dbd addr=0x40f0f8 brief and full\n- fw_get_function_source md5=23e3796091277a8be1e69dc8f5093dbd addr=0x407ff8 (main) brief/full\n- fw_get_surface AS-004\n- identification full? Let me do the main reading first in parallel.","stream":true,"block":0}

