data: {"seq":70,"ts":"2026-08-23T09:07:32.779Z","text":"dhcp6s: sub_40F0F8 is `sys_run_command` at 0x40f0f8: `vsprintf(s[260], format, arg); return system(s);` — a format+system wrapper. The question is whether attacker-controlled data reaches the `format` argument with a format string or unsanitized command. This is the classic dhcp6s (KAME) \"dhcp6c\" known vuln: CVE-2011-... Actually the KAME dhcp6s has a well-known vuln: dhcp6_ctl_readcommand → base64 decode of ctl command → system() with command injection? There is a known TP-Link dhcp6s vuln? Hmm.\n\nLet me look at the callers of sub_40F0F8 (sys_run_command) to see what paths call it with what arguments. Use fw_call_trace direction=in, name=sub_40F0F8.\n\nAlso main's memcpy lines 366/377: `memcpy(&unk_43C628, pai->ai_addr, pai->ai_addrlen)` — global buffers, getaddrinfo-controlled length; typically ai_addrlen is bounded by the addr family (16 for IPv4, 28 for IPv6); the global buffer might be smaller — need to check sizes of unk_43C628/unk_43C60C. That's more of an info concern.\n\nFocus: system() reachable from network? dhcp6s has a control channel: dhcp6c/dhcp6s use a Unix-domain socket? No — KAME dhcp6s uses a TCP control port? In this Archer firmware, IN-004 says dhcp6s binds TCP. The control protocol: dhcp6_ctl_readcommand reads lines, base64-decodes, then dispatches commands. There's a known vulnerability class: dhcp6s command injection via ctl socket (unauthenticated?). But need to confirm the ctl socket is the public TCP input.\n\nLet me trace callers of sub_40F0F8 within dhcp6s. And also get full source of main to understand which path leads to sys_run_command. Let me:\n1. fw_call_t","stream":true,"block":0}

