data: {"seq":92,"ts":"2026-08-23T09:07:40.205Z","text":"dhcp6s main:\n- Binds UDP 547 (insock) + UDP 546 (outsock) for DHCPv6 relay/server.\n- Control channel: dhcp6_ctl_init(off_43C070, off_43C074, 5, &dword_43C060) — ctl port comes from -p option; default off_43C074. Then select loop: sub_4065B4() handles insock reads; dhcp6_ctl_acceptcommand(dword_43C060, sub_404BE0) handles ctl connection; dhcp6_ctl_readcommand(&readfds) dispatches ctl commands.\n- The dispatch: sys_run_command (sub_40F0F8) is in the dispatcher list — likely called from dhcp6_ctl_readcommand / sub_424080 / sub_402660 with attacker-controlled command from the ctl socket (base64-decoded).\n\nThe call_trace for sub_40F0F8 returned only a hint — no callers listed (probably not resolved in graph). I need to find callers of sub_40F0F8 by reading functions: sub_424080, sub_402660, dhcp6_ctl_readcommand (0x4182b8), dhcp6_ctl_acceptcommand (0x418504), and sub_404BE0.\n\nThis is essentially KAME dhcp6s. Known: dhcp6s control protocol is authenticated via HMAC (dhcp6_ctl_authinit with key file -k). But in this TP-Link firmware, default config? The ctl port off_43C074 default may be 0 (auth off?) — \"if dword_43C608 (auth init ok) ... else skip opening control port\". So control port opens only if auth init succeeded (i.e., a key file provided). Hmm, but off_43C070/off_43C074 defaults?\n\nRather than go down this rabbit hole, let me check what calls sys_run_command. Let me read sub_424080 and sub_402660 and dhcp6_ctl_readcommand. Also sub_404BE0 (the handler passed to ctl accept).\n\nAlso consider the more classic TCP surface: identification says dhcp6s IN-004 is TCP with port None; actual is UDP 547/546 and a TCP ctl port. The DHCPv6 client packet parsing: sub_4065B4() — receives DHCPv6 messages from insock (recvmsg) and parses. That's the network input parser with base64? No.\n\nPriority: find where sys_run_command's format is constructed from network/client data. Let me read the dispatchers.","stream":true,"block":0}

