data: {"seq":153,"ts":"2026-08-23T09:08:13.896Z","text":"sub_424080/sub_402660 are init/atexit style functions (function table iteration) — not sys_run_command callers. The \"dispatch\" tag was bogus. sub_4065B4 is the DHCPv6 message handler: recvmsg → dhcp6_get_options etc. The network input path: DHCPv6 client packets arrive via recvmsg and are parsed by dhcp6_get_options; the whole handler is 825 lines.\n\nWhere is sys_run_command called? Not found via call_graph. Let me search for functions in dhcp6s that reference command execution: fw_search pattern \"sys_run\" or look at strings? fw_browse_firmware can read... but dhcp6s is in squashfs, not a text file. \n\nAlternative: find which function calls sub_40F0F8 by searching callees. Try fw_call_trace direction=out on a big parser? Actually call_trace returned just a hint for in-direction — the graph may not have caller edges for this function (decompile gaps). \n\nBetter: grep the decompiled data via fw_search with pattern matching names like \"cmd\", \"run\", \"exec\", \"popen\", \"system\". fw_search(pattern) searches functions/files in the code graph by name pattern. Let me search \"cmd\" and \"system\" in this job.\n\nAlso, the ctl handler sub_404BE0 calls cfparse — config parse on received command? cfparse is the config file parser (KAME dhcp6s parses /etc/dhcp6s.conf) — called from main at startup with the -c config path. In sub_404BE0 (ctl command handler) cfparse is called — KAME dhcp6s supports a control command \"reload\" that re-parses config. Not obviously attacker-controlled command execution.\n\nActually wait — the real KAME dhcp6s ctl protocol: client connects, sends a line like \"reload\" or \"advertise\". The handler validates MAC. In TP-Link firmware the ctl port may be open by default? main: `if (dword_43C608) { dhcp6_ctl_init(...) } else { skip opening control port }`. dword_43C608 is set by dhcp6_ctl_authinit(off_43C07C[0], ...), which uses the -k ctlkeyfile. If auth init fails → control port skipped. Default key file off_43C07C[0] is NULL → auth init probably fails → no ctl port. Hmm, so ctl channel is likely closed by default. Unless dhcp6_ctl_authinit returns 0 with NULL key (i.e., no auth). Hard to know statically.\n\nRather than chase sys_run_command reachability in dhcp6s deeper, consider the other P0 paths and the DHCPv6 message parser itself (sub_4065B4, 825 lines; dhcp6_get_options). That's UDP 547 input — a real network parser. But dynamic via net isn't available for UDP (trace tool is TCP/HTTP-oriented), though via=stdin can feed bytes to the binary? dhcp6s doesn't read from stdin; it would just fail at interface init (ifinit). Hmm — dhcp6s needs a real interface (br0?) and /tmp/dhcp6s_duid etc. Under qemu-user it'd exit early. Fuzzing dhcp6_get_options with qemu fuzz (whole-binary mode) would be blocked by ifinit/config setup.\n\nBetter idea: use fw_request_fuzz function-targeted on dhcp6_get_options? Function-targeted fuzz needs arm/x86 \"with a concrete hypothesis\" — this is MIPS, so whole-binary preferred; \"preferred on MIPS: qemu persistent handshake often fails\". Whole-binary fuzz of dhcp6s would need it to reach the parser — it won't (needs interface).\n\nAlternative attack surface more amenable to stdin/file differential: \n- httpd (:80) — routes via fw_routes; handlers parse GET/POST; could trace via net port 80 (request_path, payload). That's network but allowed now (\"继续挖掘\").\n- dropbear (:22) — SSH KEX parsing; earlier instruction said don't hit :80/:445; dropbear is 22. \n- vsftpd (:21) — FTP commands parse from socket; trace via net port 21 with payload \"USER ...\".\n- minidlnad — UPnP/HTTP on port 8200? identification says TCP, port null in IN-008. minidlnad parses file metadata (libavformat) — file input! It reads media files via libavformat/libexif/libid3tag. input_path trace on minidlnad is awkward (needs config).\n\nThe user's earlier focus was network ports :80/:445 for the *capability* turn. Now mining resumed, so httpd/dropbear/vsftpd are legit targets. Among them, the surest dynamic path: vsftpd on port 21 — payload \"USER x\\r\\n\" via net. Or httpd port 80 — request_path + payload.\n\nBut honestly, the strongest candidates so far from attack-surface P0s are in binaries I haven't mapped: 08b0860102c4b034bfbae2238115a7e6 (network_thread strcpy@0x4114d0:287) and 2c508f48e714f8276fee71652f19b4ba (main with many strcpy). Which binaries are these? They weren't in the visible identification list. Let me identify them: I can use fw_list_binaries (truncated last time — it cut at \"0098fba848\" mid list; wait, it cut off lib*.so entries? Actually it showed up to lib/libmsglog? no. It showed \"0098fba848\" truncated). Let me instead probe: fw_get_function_source with md5 08b08601... addr 0x4114d0 will return binary_path! Same for 2c508f48... addr 0x400e28. That identifies the binaries. Do that, plus check fw_routes for httpd.\n\nAlso for the AI-review hint \"hunt_next\" - not user-facing.\n\nLet me get binary paths for the two unknown md5s, and read their brief sources.","stream":false,"block":0}

