data: {"seq":190,"ts":"2026-08-23T09:08:28.920Z","text":"Very important findings:\n\n1. dhcp6s cmdi path 165af590169e4972: the chain is main → get_duid (0x40f1d0) → sub_40F168 → sub_40F0F8 (sys_run_command). get_duid uses `dprintf(3, \"server6_init\", ...)` and sub_40F168 has asrc=serial (get_duid reads DUID from file then sys_run_command? KAME dhcp6s get_duid: generates DUID by calling \"ifconfig\" or \"uname\"? Actually KAME's get_duid runs `/sbin/ifconfig` via sys_run_command? No — KAME get_duid: if /etc/dhcp6s_duid exists read it; else call get_duid... which executes `sys_run_command(\"ifconfig %s\", ifname)`? Hmm. In KAME, get_duid does run \"ifconfig inet6 ...\" commands. But the DUID is derived from MAC address via ifconfig output — not attacker-controlled via network; the \"attack surface\" flags asrc serial (hardware serial). So the dhcp6s P0 cmdi chain is main(argv) → get_duid(serial/file) → sub_40F168 → sys_run_command: format strings are constant-ish (\"ifconfig...\"), not network-controlled. The verdict: not a real cmdi from network. Reachability static-only, format not attacker-controlled → I'd NOT record this as vuln without evidence.\n\n2. xl2tpd network_thread: line 287 strcpy((char*)(j+916), v10) — actually looking at the code, v10 = strerror(v8) where v8 = -packet (read_packet error). strerror() of a small int → bounded short string, copied into tunnel struct at offset 916. v8 = -packet where packet is read_packet return; read errors are errno values (bounded). strerror output is short (< 50 bytes). Target offset 916 in a tunnel struct — likely an error string field sized ~ enough. Not attacker-controlled content (errno-derived). Not a vuln.\n\nSo xl2tpd line 287 isn't a real bug: v10 comes from strerror().\n\nBoth P0s are AI","stream":true,"block":0}

