data: {"seq":198,"ts":"2026-08-23T09:08:32.023Z","text":"Very important findings:\n\n1. dhcp6s cmdi path 165af590169e4972: the chain is main → get_duid (0x40f1d0) → sub_40F168 → sub_40F0F8 (sys_run_command). get_duid uses `dprintf(3, \"server6_init\", ...)` and sub_40F168 has asrc=serial (get_duid reads DUID from file then sys_run_command? KAME dhcp6s get_duid: generates DUID by calling \"ifconfig\" or \"uname\"? Actually KAME's get_duid runs `/sbin/ifconfig` via sys_run_command? No — KAME get_duid: if /etc/dhcp6s_duid exists read it; else call get_duid... which executes `sys_run_command(\"ifconfig %s\", ifname)`? Hmm. In KAME, get_duid does run \"ifconfig inet6 ...\" commands. But the DUID is derived from MAC address via ifconfig output — not attacker-controlled via network; the \"attack surface\" flags asrc serial (hardware serial). So the dhcp6s P0 cmdi chain is main(argv) → get_duid(serial/file) → sub_40F168 → sys_run_command: format strings are constant-ish (\"ifconfig...\"), not network-controlled. The verdict: not a real cmdi from network. Reachability static-only, format not attacker-controlled → I'd NOT record this as vuln without evidence.\n\n2. xl2tpd network_thread: line 287 strcpy((char*)(j+916), v10) — actually looking at the code, v10 = strerror(v8) where v8 = -packet (read_packet error). strerror() of a small int → bounded short string, copied into tunnel struct at offset 916. v8 = -packet where packet is read_packet return; read errors are errno values (bounded). strerror output is short (< 50 bytes). Target offset 916 in a tunnel struct — likely an error string field sized ~ enough. Not attacker-controlled content (errno-derived). Not a vuln.\n\nSo xl2tpd line 287 isn't a real bug: v10 comes from strerror().\n\nBoth P0s are AI-flagged false positives, sadly. The AI review reason for xl2tpd said \"v10 可能来自网络\" — but actual code shows v10 = strerror(v8) bounded.\n\nNow I need to find REAL vulnerabilities. Better approach: exploit the dynamic capability we validated. The stdin/file differential works, and net traces (port) presumably work too (HTTP GET or payload on port). The strongest candidates: \n\n- httpd (9582aa23): HTTP handlers on :80. fw_routes gives handlers. Known TP-Link Archer C7 httpd has many command-injection/buffer-overflow CVEs. Let me pull routes and dig into handlers, then reproduce via port-80 traces.\n- minidlnad (3e3258c9): UPnP/HTTP on port 8200 + media file parsing (libavformat) — file input via input_path could be powerful (media parsing of crafted files). minidlnad config in /etc/minidlna.conf; running it under qemu needs the conf; but whole-binary fuzz? Hmm.\n- vsftpd (78023622): FTP :21 — command parsing. Standard vsftpd 2.x; if this is an old version, known bugs. But Archer's vsftpd is likely stock.\n- dropbear (831411ed): SSH :22 KEX parsing — known CVE targets (CVE-2012-0920? CVE-2016-7406-7408 old dropbear). This Archer firmware dropbear version? If old, buffer overflows in KEX. Port 22 trace with payloads_hex multi-packet could differential-test.\n\nBut honestly, the highest-value, most-likely-real bugs in Archer C7 firmware are in httpd (many documented CVEs for TP-Link Archer C7: CVE-2018-... command injection in cgi). Let me check fw_routes for httpd.\n\nAlso, let me reconsider: the user's workflow emphasis (system prompt) is: dynamic verification — identify preferred network-entry binary_md5 → fw_request_trace (port for network; request_path for HTTP; payloads_hex multi-packet). Let me get routes + surfaces for httpd (AS-014) and minidlnad (AS-008).\n\nBatch:\n1. fw_routes binary_md5=9582aa23b0bc2de099b9997b09457d01 limit=100\n2. fw_get_surface AS-014 (httpd)\n3. fw_get_surface AS-008 (minidlnad)","stream":true,"block":0}

