data: {"seq":219,"ts":"2026-08-23T09:08:37.824Z","text":"minidlnad (3e3258c907b4b461c90472e1fcd6cddb) is a very promising target:\n- Dispatchers include three `sys_run_command` variants: sub_405594 (0x405594), sub_405D5C (0x405d5c), sub_416168 (0x416168) — command execution sinks reachable from dispatching!\n- Plus real network surfaces: upnphttp, SSDP, and file-based media parsing via `sub_42412C` with carriers \"file content buffer → a1 (read fd=fd)\" and \"fread\" into v11/v7/v8 at 0x449b64 (media thumbnail parsing: SendResp_thumbnail/albumArt/resizedimg parse JPEG files — jpeg_read_header is in the parses list).\n\nminidlnad is a well-known source of CVEs (MiniDLNA before 1.1.5: SSDP buffer overflow CVE-2013-...; actually CVE-2013-...? There's the famous \"buffer overflow in ProcessSSDPRequest\" etc.)\n\nPlan for minidlnad:\n1. Read the sys_run_command variants (sub_405594, sub_405D5C, sub_416168) to see their format strings; find callers to see if attacker-controlled (e.g., album art path from HTTP request → command?).\n2. Read sub_404120 and sub_450CE0 (dispatchers).\n3. Look for strcpy/sscanf issues in the HTTP/SSDP path: ProcessSSDPRequest (0x4218a0), sub_40D1FC (HTTP request parser? rio/read), ParseNameValue (0x41c8f8), parsexml (0x41d8d8).\n4. Check sub_42412C (final handler: reads file content, carrier \"file content buffer → a1\").\n\nAlso! An important angle: minidlnad reads media files (album art thumbnails, resized images via libjpeg). The carrier bindings show sub_449b64 (fread ×3) and sub_42412C (read fd=fd) — file-input parsing. We can use input_path=/tmp/poc.bin with a crafted JPEG to differential-test the file parser! And qemu_exec to crash-check.\n\nAlso SSDP: UDP 1900 typically. minidlnad listens UPnP HTTP port 8200 and SSDP ","stream":true,"block":0}

