data: {"seq":253,"ts":"2026-08-23T09:08:54.297Z","text":"Analysis:\n\n- sub_405594 (sys_run_command variant 1): builds v5[8196] with snprintf then system(v5) — need to see snprintf format; callees: CreateDatabase, start_scanner, etc. Likely `system(\"mkdir ...\")` — init-time, not network.\n- sub_416168 (variant 3): v7[256]; snprintf(v7...) then system(v7) with inet_ntoa input — this looks like the SSDP/HTTP announce: e.g. \"echo ... > /tmp/upnpd?\" Hmm inet_ntoa(a1) — it takes a primary client IP? Let me read full.\n- sub_405D5C: options/config parsing (readoptionsfile) — not network-facing directly (config file from flash), line 379/382 system(v43) where v43 is realpath() output. Not attacker-controlled via network HTTP.\n- ProcessSSDPRequest: recvfrom(a1, v36, 0x5DB, ...) — buffer 1499 bytes, reads SSDP; parses with strcasestrc, strtol, sub_420570, sub_4205F0, SearchClientCache, sub_420E40 (net_open_connection). This is the classic SSDP handling. Historical MiniDLNA SSDP vulnerabilities: CVE-2013-7469? Actually that's \"MiniUPnPd\" buffer overflow. For MiniDLNA: CVE-2012-??? Hmm, \"minidlna 1.0.25\" had CVE-2013-3797? I recall MiniDLNA < 1.1.3 had a heap overflow in ProcessSSDPRequest (SSDP_Get_Location?). Firmware-specific anyway; we analyze this binary.\n\n- sub_40D1FC: HTTP request handler (parse URL) — memmove(dest, src, len) line 68, memcpy modelnumber etc. This is the UPnP HTTP parser dispatcher (routes to SendResp_* handlers).\n- sub_42412C: final handler — read(fd, a1, a2) reads a file into a1 with a2 bytes (buffer from caller). This is the \"served file\" function; a2 presumably the file size... if the buffer is smaller than file size → overflow. It's in carrier bindings \"file content buffer → a1\".\n\nThe most testable dynamic surfaces:\n1. **HTTP :8200** (minidlnad UPnP) — we can trace via net with port 8200 (need the real port). Identification says port null for IN-008. minidlnad default UPnP port 8200; SSDP 1900 UDP. Let me check the config in rootfs (/etc/minidlna.conf) via fw_browse_firmware to find the actual port. That also confirms how it's launched (argv) — which matters for qemu_exec/trace argv.\n\n2. **Media file parsing** (jpeg via libjpeg for thumbnails): file input — input_path traces. But the parsing entry (sub_449b64 fread, 0x42412c read) requires the right URL path to trigger image processing (SendResp_resizedimg/thumbnail → resize via libjpeg). Under qemu with a crafted JPEG file and proper request, we could differential-test. That requires more setup (config + db).\n\nRather than the minidlnad rabbit hole, let me FIRST check what config exists and how minidlnad is started (init scripts in rootfs): fw_browse_firmware etc/etc/init.d/... Then decide.\n\nAlso strong idea: use **fw_request_fuzz whole-binary** on minidlnad? It reads network/config — fuzz would thrash. Not ideal.\n\nLet me check minidlna config/startup in firmware: browse etc dir. And read sub_416168 full (42 lines, SSDP announce → system) + ProcessSSDPRequest full (368 lines) to hunt for memcpy/strcpy overflows in SSDP parse path, and sub_420570/sub_4205F0 (SSDP header parse helpers).\n\nAlso read sub_405594 full to see snprintf format (mkdir/rm -rf of media dirs—could involve config path from flash; not network).\n\nPriority: the SSDP path is network-reachable (UDP 1900) and pre-auth. MiniDLNA ProcessSSDPRequest historically: buffer overflow in \"SearchClientCache\"? Let me read ProcessSSDPRequest full + sub_420570 + sub_4205F0.\n\nBatch:\n1. fw_browse_firmware path etc (find init scripts/config)\n2. sub_416168 full (42 lines)\n3. ProcessSSDPRequest full (368 lines)\n4. sub_4205F0 brief (helper after SSDP parse)","stream":true,"block":0}

