data: {"seq":269,"ts":"2026-08-23T09:08:58.926Z","text":"ProcessSSDPRequest analysis:\n- v36[1500] on stack, recvfrom max 0x5DB=1499 → OK.\n- M-SEARCH parsing: bounds-checked loops within v35. st: v24, mx... Then sub_4205F0() is called to format the M-SEARCH response (builds reply with sprintf into some buffer, then sendto). sub_420570(v15>>20) sets a random delay.\n- NOTIFY parsing: haystack=LOCATION value; m=SERVER; j=NT; k=NTS. Checks \"ssdp:alive\", NT MediaRenderer, then matches SERVER \"Allegro-Software-RomPlug\" or LOCATION contains \"SamsungMRDesc.xml\" or SERVER contains \"DigiOn DiXiM\" → SearchClientCache(...) then sub_420E40(haystack) — net_open_connection(LOCATION URL) — the minidlna acts as SSDP client fetching device description from LOCATION URL! That's a client-side fetch: URL-controlled by remote attacker → SSRF / parser input into the HTTP client (upnphttp get). This is a classic attack: malicious NOTIFY with LOCATION pointing to attacker's HTTP server → minidlna fetches & parses arbitrary XML (pupnp ico parsing?). Historical MiniDLNA SSDP NOTIFY handling had CVEs (","stream":true,"block":0}

