data: {"seq":282,"ts":"2026-08-23T09:09:08.410Z","text":"ProcessSSDPRequest analysis:\n- v36[1500] on stack, recvfrom max 0x5DB=1499 → OK.\n- M-SEARCH parsing: bounds-checked loops within v35. st: v24, mx... Then sub_4205F0() is called to format the M-SEARCH response (builds reply with sprintf into some buffer, then sendto). sub_420570(v15>>20) sets a random delay.\n- NOTIFY parsing: haystack=LOCATION value; m=SERVER; j=NT; k=NTS. Checks \"ssdp:alive\", NT MediaRenderer, then matches SERVER \"Allegro-Software-RomPlug\" or LOCATION contains \"SamsungMRDesc.xml\" or SERVER contains \"DigiOn DiXiM\" → SearchClientCache(...) then sub_420E40(haystack) — net_open_connection(LOCATION URL) — the minidlna acts as SSDP client fetching device description from LOCATION URL! That's a client-side fetch: URL-controlled by remote attacker → SSRF / parser input into the HTTP client (upnphttp get). This is a classic attack: malicious NOTIFY with LOCATION pointing to attacker's HTTP server → minidlna fetches & parses arbitrary XML (pupnp ico parsing?). Historical MiniDLNA SSDP NOTIFY handling had CVEs (e.g. CVE-2013-3797? no...). Anyway sub_420E40 net_open_connection with attacker URL is interesting but requires attacker to be a server.\n\n- Notice ProcessSSDPRequest's dangerous_calls listed only recvfrom & log_err — no memcpy/strcpy. The M-SEARCH reply builder sub_4205F0 might have the sprintf overflow! Classic MiniDLNA bug: in `ProcessSSDPRequest`/`sub_4205F0`, building \"HTTP/1.1 200 OK...\" response with ST string into a fixed buffer. Let me read sub_4205F0 and sub_420570.\n\nsub_416168: `snprintf(v7, 256, \"isgnsta %d\", a1.s_addr); system(v7)` — command injection? a1.s_addr is IP address (numeric from in_addr). \"%d\" of s_addr — an integer, not a string; can't inject shell metacharacters... unless a2-related stuff. Wait, actually it's `%d` — prints the numeric value. No injection. Exit code interpreted for guest-network check. Not exploitable.\n\nsub_405594: (first sys_run_command) likely startup \"mkdir -p /tmp/minidlna/db\" etc. Not network.\n\nCurrent best leads:\n1. sub_4205F0 (SSDP response builder) — potential sprintf overflow with ST value (attacker-controlled, up to v24 length but v24 bounded by v35 in v36[1500]... ST \"ssdp:...\" matching requires v33 to match off_4718AC entries or \"ssdp:all\" — restricted, so ST value is not arbitrary length. Actually sub_4205F0 receives i (index into off_4718AC) → ST string is from the static list, length bounded. So likely safe-ish. But check code.\n\n2. sub_420E40 net_open_connection(LOCATION) — the SSDP client fetch: attacker sends NOTIFY with LOCATION=http://attacker/xml → minidlna connects and parses. Under qemu dynamic trace we can't easily emulate a real network, BUT the HTTP client parser (upnphttp / HttpClient) could be reached via... hmm. Actually minidlna's HTTP client reads the device description XML and parses with parsexml (0x41d8d8). XML parsing of attacker file = file-based parser! We could potentially feed the XML parser via stdin/file input_path trace! parsexml @0x41d8d8.\n\n3. The media file parsers: album art/resized image via libjpeg (jpeg_read_header), libavformat, libexif — when serving /resized_img or /albumArt URLs with a crafted image file on the media dir. That's file-input based (input_path). This is a big attack surface (image parsing libraries): libjpeg 9, libavcodec 54 (ffmpeg 1.x era — old ffmpeg has many known bugs), libtiff, libexif 12.3.3","stream":true,"block":0}

