data: {"seq":322,"ts":"2026-08-23T09:09:24.089Z","text":"The rootfs access: `etc` dir exists under the jail root — the earlier `path=\"etc\"` returned empty entries, odd. Maybe \"etc\" at top level is different from the squashfs-root/etc. The jail root seems to be the analysis workspace root (with emba.log, firmware/, etc.), not the squashfs root. So browse \"firmware/binwalk_extracted/firmware.extracted/100000/squashfs-root/etc\".\n\nsub_4205F0: snprintf + sendto — the M-SEARCH response. Uses a6 index (ST from static list) so bounded. Need full to confirm no %s of attacker data without length — a7 = lan_addr string, fine.\n\nsub_420E40 (net_open_connection): 177 lines; read(fd, &v20[v16], 0x1FFF - v16) where v20[8192] presumably; v16 = accumulated content length tracked; reads up to 0x1FFF. Bounded by 8192 stack buffer? v20[v16] with v16 < 0x2000. OK-ish. It parses HTTP response with ParseNameValue (0x41c8f8) and GetValueFromNameValueList — the response from attacker-controlled URL. Known MiniDLNA bugs in Http_Get_Connection/parse: \"ParseNameValue\" buffer handling. parsexml is mini wrapper.\n\nNow — this SSDP-driven HTTP client path is dynamic-testable only through network emulation, which our net trace tool can't do for UDP 1900 (it's TCP). Let me not sink more into minidlnad network flow.\n\nBetter dynamic angle: **media file parsers** (the carrier bindings): sub_449b64 (fread ×3) and sub_42412C (read fd) — these are reached when minidlnad serves /resized_img or /albumArt or /media item with image format, invoking libjpeg/libavformat/exif. As FILE parses, we can drive them with input_path via stdin? No — input_path drops a file; minidlnad parses files given a path from the request URL / DB. Running full minidlnad under qemu-user needs config & db... it can run without DB? probe: `minidlnad -f /etc/minidlna.conf`? It would scan? Actually with \"-R\" it rescans; without, it uses existing db. It opens UPnP sockets — qemu-user can open sockets (localhost). Might work but heavy.\n\nAlternative: focus on **httpd** — the web server with 4882 functions decompiled, port 80 reachable via our net trace tool (HTTP). TP-Link Archer C7 httpd is the classic target (command injection via cgi params, auth bypass, buffer overflows in cookie handling). fw_routes returned 0 routes (no route table), but we have the surface: httpDispatcher @0x425d08, parsers sub_420B48 (HTTP request line parse?) & httpBase64decode & httpLineUnescape & STRTOK_REENTRANT...\n\nActually — first, let me widen with the attack-surface query for httpd binary specifically (source=network, sink=memunsafe/fmt/cmdexec, binary filter?). fw_attack_surface doesn't have binary filter, but we can filter by scanning results for binary_md5 9582aa23. Let me run fw_attack_surface with verified_only=false, source=network, sink=memunsafe, limit=50, and look for httpd paths (9582aa23). That might take a while but it's the systematic way.\n\nAlso read sub_420B48 (HTTP request parser) full and httpDispatcher full (0x425d08) to understand routing & find handler table → cmdexec sinks.\n\nLet me do:\n1. fw_attack_surface limit=50 source=network sink=memunsafe (already have some; but specifically want httpd) — heavy; instead query sink=fmt for httpd? We can't filter binary. Let me just pull sink=cmdexec with source=network full list? Earlier cmdexec query gave only 2 paths total (dhcp6s & start_pppd). So cmdexec network paths are exhausted: dhcp6s (bogus) & xl2tpd start_pppd (unclear). So command-injection from network is not fertile.\n\nLet me query sink=memunsafe source=network with limit=50 and brief=false only for interesting ones? The earlier brief query gave P0s. Let me look at remaining paths in that list (truncated at 50). Query with sink=memunsafe, source=network, limit=50, brief=true — we saw ~14; need more. But scanning 50 brief paths is a lot of reading. Focus instead on httpd handlers directly via fw_get_function_source for httpDispatcher (0x425d08) full, sub_420B48 full.\n\n2. httpd: sub_420B48 full, httpDispatcher full, httpBase64decode full (0x42faf4).\n\nLet me batch these three reads.","stream":false,"block":0}

