earch for functions calling system in httpd.\n\nfw_search \"system\" would return too many. fw_search \"popen\"? Let me search \"exec\" or use the attack surface sink=cmdexec source=network — done, only 2 paths (both bogus). Hmm, so code-graph based cmdexec reachability is limited (decompile gaps 4186). The cgi handlers might have cmdexec via indirect calls (unresolved).\n\nAlternative pragmatic approach: **dynamic differential on httpd port 80** — just fire traces at different URLs and see which produce non-empty diffs, indicating handler dispatch. That gives us confirmation of reachability, and then we read the handler.\n\nLet me do that: fw_request_trace with httpd md5, port 80, request_path=\"/\" first? The tool: \"via=net (default when port is set) sends HTTP GET or payload on the port\". For HTTP, do NOT pass request_path=\"/\" on non-HTTP ports; on HTTP port it's fine. Baseline = no request? The diff is baseline vs trigger.\n\nPlan: \n- Trace A: port=80, request_path=\"/\", method GET (payload empty). \n- Trace B: port=80, request_path=\"/userRpm/LoginRpm.htm?Save=Save\", maybe LoginRpmHtm.\n- Trace C: port=80, request_path=\"/cgi-bin/...\" etc.\n\nBut httpd under qemu may need to bind 80 on localhost — qemu-user runs as normal user on the sandbox; binding :80 might work unprivileged? qemu-user doesn't restrict ports (no privilege check in user-mode). Also httpd likely needs /tmp config (root web dir /web? etc). It might run and serve from squashfs-root/web.\n\nLet's try the first HTTP trace and see. Also do a stdin-based trace for httpd? httpd reads stdin? No.\n\nAlso, NOTE: request_path \"/\" might be rejected (\"Do not pass request_path=\"/\" on non-HTTP ports\" — 80 IS HTTP, fine).\n\nLet me fire: fw_request_trace httpd md5=9582aa23b0bc2de099b9997b09457d01 port=80 request_path=\"/userRpm/LoginRpm.htm?Save=Save\". And in parallel poll nothing.\n\nActually wait: budget limits — fw_request_trace has \"Budget max 192\" (shared?). 432 traces exist already; budget 192 may already be consumed?? The platform hints say budgeted but traces succeeded in this session (f55640905b63). Let's just try.\n\nAlso let me fetch identification IN-014 entry to see the exact httpd invocation (init script) — this informs argv for traces (e.g., \"-n\"?). The init scripts live in squashfs-root/etc/init.d or rcS. Browse: firmware/binwalk_extracted/.../squashfs-root/etc/init.d.\n\nBatch:\n1. fw_browse_f","stream":true,"block":0}

