data: {"seq":368,"ts":"2026-08-23T09:09:55.235Z","text":"Interesting: /etc has dhcp6sctlkey and dhcp6cctlkey — so the DHCPv6 ctl channel HAS a key file on this firmware → the ctl control port may indeed be open by default (auth key present). This revives the dhcp6s ctl command path: the ctl socket accepts commands authenticated with the shared key (if the key is shipped in the firmware, an attacker can read it or it's trivial). But that's the KAME dhcp6s control protocol, and commands like \"reload\"/\"advertise\" — does a command reach sys_run_command? sys_run_command is called from sub_40F168 (which takes serial/file source, per the chain get_duid? no — chain was main→get_duid→sub_40F168→sub_40F0F8). Hmm, actually the chain shows get_duid calls sub_40F168. Let me check what sub_40F168 does — it might be \"get iface MAC via sysctl/cmd\". Not ctl-socket related.\n\nWait — also rc.d dir for init scripts. Let me check etc/rc.d to see how httpd/minidlnad/dhcp6s launch. Also the httpd trace 65875b654785 is running - poll it.","stream":false,"block":0}

