data: {"seq":438,"ts":"2026-08-23T09:10:21.244Z","text":"Trace 65875b654785 finished with status ok_empty_diff, via net port 80, diff_functions 0. So httpd on :80 ran but the request produced no function-level diff vs baseline — meaning either httpd under qemu-user didn't actually process the HTTP request (maybe it started serving but baseline==trigger because httpd's main loop is identical), or it exited before accept.\n\nBaseline vs trigger identical (0 diff): httpd main loop probably blocked in accept() and the harness's GET request didn't dispatch anything new — possible causes: httpd requires /tmp config or fails at startup; or qemu-user network to localhost:80 worked but handler code is same for baseline? Baseline probably = no request at all, so diff should show request handling functions... unless httpd failed at startup (e.g., bind fails) in both runs and never served.\n\nWe need to confirm httpd actually binds and processes. Since diff 0, the dynamic probe wasn't productive yet. httpd may need /tmp/web or such. Let me check the httpd main briefly? httpd is huge (4882 funcs).\n\nDifferent strategy: focus effort where a REAL memory-safety bug is visible statically + dynamically testable via stdin/file, per the job's strength. The most promising remaining item: the minidlnad *file parsers* (libjpeg/libavformat/libexif reading crafted media over input_path). But driving them needs minidlnad to run with config+DB — heavy.\n\nHold on — alternative: **libavformat/libavcodec/libjpeg/libexif are SHARED LIBRARIES**, and we can run one of the binaries that link them and parse files. Which binaries use libavformat? minidlnad (libavformat.so.54). Also is there `ffmpeg` binary? Not in list. minidlnad's file parsers are internal (ff_* funcs in libavformat are for network protocols — used by minidlnad? Minidlna uses av_open_input_file to probe media files (container parsing) — yes it links libavformat for media probing (use avformat_open_input on the media file → parses container!). So a crafted media file fed to minidlnad → avformat parsing → could hit the ff_udp/rtsp funcs only if URL is like \"rtsp://...\" — but local file paths use file protocol (ff_file_open). The buggy-looking funcs (ff_rtsp_connect, udp_open) aren't on the local-file path.\n\nWhere's the real parsing? libavformat's ff_avienc? For the *media library parsing* (metadata: id3tag, exif, flac, tiff, jpeg) — the known dangerous functions run when minidlnad scans/serves media: jpeg_read_header (libjpeg9), libexif 12.3.3 (old, has CVEs: CVE-2017-10811 etc), libtiff5, libid3tag.\n\nGiven the difficulty of full minidlnad setup, consider what we can dynamically hit *directly*:\n\nOption A: minidlnad whole-binary via net on its UPnP HTTP port (default 8200): the HTTP parser (sub_40D1FC) is reachable pre-auth? UPnP SOAP requires auth? MiniDLNA access via HTTP is unauthenticated (network-wide). We could trace:\n- port=8200, request_path=\"/\" (UPnP root desc)\n- Actually request_path with GET /rootDesc.xml etc.\nBut we don't know binary binds 8200 by default; config may set port. minidlnad default UPnP port 8200, SSDP 1900. Let me check its config in firmware: /etc/minidlna.conf? Not in /etc listing (no minidlna.conf). Config likely in /tmp (generated). So main default port 8200.\n\nOption B: Use fw_request_fuzz whole-binary on minidlnad with args to force a parse of stdin? minidlnad doesn't parse stdin.\n\nOption C: Revisit attack surface for other binaries with FILE sources — 182 file sources! These are config parsers reading files — reachable via input_path! e.g., httpd reads /etc configs? dnsproxy reads /tmp/dnsproxy.conf? The identification's file sources belong to various daemons reading configs from flash (/etc) or /tmp.\n\nRather than continue guessing, let me query the attack surface filtered to source=file + sink=memunsafe, brief, and look for short chains in small daemons (dnsproxy, ipcserver, uclited, bpalogin). The fw_update sink (1 path!) is interesting: source? Let me check \"fwupdate\" sink — TP-Link firmware update path (often in httpd: firmware upgrade with md5 check). Only 1 path.\n\nAlso serial sources (20) — radartool? \n\nPlan: \n1. attack_surface: sink=memunsafe, source=file, brief, limit 30 → scan for candidates in small binaries.\n2. attack_surface: sink=fwupdate (limit 10) → the fw update path.\n\nDo both in parallel.","stream":false,"block":0}

