data: {"seq":467,"ts":"2026-08-23T09:10:32.069Z","text":"file→memunsafe returns 0 paths and fwupdate returns 0 (the filters didn't match; the summary totals are global). The attack surface query with source=file filter finds none — the file sources don't have resolved memunsafe paths in graph.\n\nLet me step back and take a more pragmatic approach. \n\n**What can we actually verify dynamically with high confidence?**\n1. stdin/file differential (works).\n2. net port trace (works for TCP; httpd gave empty diff but ran).\n3. fuzz (fw_request_fuzz) whole-binary.\n4. qemu_exec crash checks.\n\n**Candidates that read stdin/files directly (drivable via stdin/input_path):**\n- busybox applets: tried md5sum/sh. Interesting ones: `tftp` client (reads net), `udhcpc`/`udhcpd` (network), `logger` (stdin), `syslogd`... These are generic busybox v1.01 from 2005-2006 — extremely old. BusyBox 1.01's applets: `cat`/`echo` trivial; the interesting parser is `msh` (mc shell, ancient with known bugs) or `linuxrc`... Hmm. \n\nActually wait — a richer idea: **httpd reads configuration/state FILES from /tmp** (e.g., /tmp/tmp_xxx or flash config). The 182 \"file\" sources are in httpd etc. Feeding files via input_path then running httpd with argv pointing at them won't work because httpd reads fixed paths.\n\nLet me reconsider the strongest actually-verifiable bug direction available in this job: **minidlnad media parsers via its real file-serving path** — no; setup heavy.\n\n**Better: use fw_request_fuzz on minidlnad to fuzz its HTTP input on port 8200?** fuzz tool is AFL++ qemu whole-binary — it starts the binary and fuzzes... with args spec [buf,len] only for function mode. Whole-binary mode: it fuzzes stdin? \"Omit function for whole-binary mode (preferred on MIPS)\". Whole-binary AFL qemu fuzzing usually targets programs reading STDIN or files. minidlnad reads network — AFL qemu needs -L? Not configurable. Not feasible.\n\n**Direct functional approach — pick a small binary with a file parser and fuzz stdin:**\nWhich binaries here parse stdin/files and have decompiled vulnerable patterns? Let me search the graph: fw_search(\"fread\")? Doesn't work that way. \n\nLet me reconsider busybox: version 1.01 (2005). Known vulnerabilities in busybox 1.01: lots (CVE-2006-1056? CVE-2015-...?). The most famous old busybox bugs: `tar` path traversal, `httpd` (busybox httpd) chunked encoding, `unzip`/`dpkg`. But our busybox has no `tar`/`unzip` applets in the defined list (ar applets: arping, brctl, cat, chmod, date, df, echo, ethreg, false, getty, ifconfig, init, insmod, kill, klogd, linuxrc, ln, logger, login, logread, ls, lsmod, mount, msh, ping, ps, reboot, rm, rmmod, route, sh, sleep, syslogd, test, tftp, top, true, udhcpc, udhcpd, umount, vconfig).\n\n`udhcpc`/`udhcpd` parse DHCP packets from network; `ping` parse args; `tftp` client parse network + args; `msh`/`sh` parse scripts (a432876827e2 showed sh script parsing with 45 diff functions!). \n\n**Shell script parsing** = the strongest dynamic diff we have (45 functions incl safe_read). Can we find a memory bug in busybox ash/msh? BusyBox 1.01 msh/sh had multiple heap overflows (e.g., CVE-2006-... in ash? \"busybox bash\" no). Probably not a clean finding.\n\n**Look at file-reading FILE sources via identification full.** Let me fetch identification full to see candidate files & get the full binary manifest mapping (the earlier fw_list_binaries output was truncated — I still don't have md5s for dnsproxy etc. Wait, I do from identification: dnsproxy 5bf87d074af47ea6dd761274cae3527b etc.)\n\nRealistic high-value small targets with stdin/file parsing to fuzz:\n- **bpalogin 5bf87d07? no — bpalogin is f8a1d1f6** (UDP, proprietary login protocol — parses server responses).\n- **dnsproxy 5bf87d07**: DNS proxy — reads DNS packets (UDP 53?) — actually dnsproxy in TP-Link reads /tmp/dnsproxy.conf and forwards DNS. DNS parsing! dnsproxy parses DNS queries (from LAN clients). DNS name decompression! Classic overflow area. Is dnsproxy stdin/testable? It reads from socket. Hmm.\n\nOK here's the thing — every juicy parser here is a network daemon, and our net trace works only for TCP (httpd empty). UDP daemons (dhcp6s UDP 547, dnsproxy UDP 53, minidlnad UDP 1900) can't be driven by via=net (it does TCP connect + payload). \n\nBUT: the identification's AS-004 said dhcp6s is \"tcp\"! Because the ctl channel is TCP. And /etc/dhcp6sctlkey exists. The **dhcp6s ctl TCP channel** IS reachable via via=net! What's the ctl port? -p option default: off_43C074. KAME dhcp6s default","stream":true,"block":0}

