w area. Is dnsproxy stdin/testable? It reads from socket. Hmm.\n\nOK here's the thing — every juicy parser here is a network daemon, and our net trace works only for TCP (httpd empty). UDP daemons (dhcp6s UDP 547, dnsproxy UDP 53, minidlnad UDP 1900) can't be driven by via=net (it does TCP connect + payload). \n\nBUT: the identification's AS-004 said dhcp6s is \"tcp\"! Because the ctl channel is TCP. And /etc/dhcp6sctlkey exists. The **dhcp6s ctl TCP channel** IS reachable via via=net! What's the ctl port? -p option default: off_43C074. KAME dhcp6s default ctlport = DHCP6CTL_PORT = 0x0691? Actually 547? In KAME, DHCP6CTL_PORT is 6969? Let me check: KAME dhcp6ctl uses port 5555? I recall dhcp6ctl_port default 5555 in some; or \"DHCP6CTL_PORT 6969\". Let me look at dhcp6_ctl_init and the -p default (off_43C074 initial value) in dhcp6s.\n\nActually the ctl protocol on this firmware: dhcp6c connects to dhcp6s ctl port. The ctl port is TCP; commands are base64-encoded with HMAC. If key is in /etc/dhcp6sctlkey (readable), we can forge commands IF we implement the protocol (HMAC-MD5 with key?). That's deep KAME work; ctl commands: \"reload\", \"advertise\", \"notify\"? Do any ctl commands run system()? sys_run_command callers in dhcp6s: only get_duid/sub_40F168 (log). Not ctl. So no cmdi through ctl.\n\nLet me pivot to concrete verifiable bug via **stdin differential on dhcp6s's actual config parser** — cfparse! dhcp6s parses its config file (config/parser: yyparse, cfparse). That's a FILE source (config), locally controlled though.\n\nI think the deepest value now: **xfuzz the library-level parsers reachable via stdin in binaries that DO read stdin as their natural interface**. Which daemons read stdin? Virtually none — they're all socket-based.\n\nWait, what about **vsftpd** (port 21 TCP) — FTP commands over TCP — reachable via net trace! Payload \"USER\\r\\n\" etc. And **dropbear** (port 22 TCP) — SSH KEX — reachable via payloads_hex multi-packet! These are TCP, our net