data: {"seq":504,"ts":"2026-08-23T09:10:48.723Z","text":"sub_40D1FC analysis (UPnP HTTP request line parser):\n\n```\nv5 = *(a1+32) // request buffer\n// 1st token (method): copy up to 15 bytes into v7[16] — bounded ok\n// skip spaces; if starts with \"http://\", advance to next '/'\n// 2nd token (URL): copy up to 511 into v8[500]?? wait v8[7]!! \n```\n\nLook: `char v8[7];` — BUT `for (i=0; i<511 && *v5!=32 && *v5!=13; ++i) v8[i] = *v5++;` copies UP TO 511 bytes into v8 which is declared as 7 bytes! That's the classic **stack buffer overflow in upnphttp.c**: URL path copied into a 7-byte buffer v8 with bound 511.\n\nWait — let's double check stack layout: \n```\nchar v7[16];   // sp+40\nchar v8[7];    // sp+50\nchar v9[2];    // sp+57\nchar v10;      // sp+59\nchar v11[2];   // sp+5A\nchar v12[500]; // sp+5C\nvoid *src;     // sp+250\n_BYTE v14[2];  // sp+254\nconst char *v15; // sp+258\n```\nv8 at sp+50, 7 bytes → occupies sp+50..sp+56. Then v9 at sp+57, v10 at sp+59, v11 at sp+5A, v12[500] at sp+5C. So writing beyond 7 bytes into v8 would overwrite v9, v10, v11, then v12 (500 bytes)... So the overflow (up to 511 bytes from URL) would smash v12 (used later as buffer for SendResp_thumbnail/dlnafile) and beyond","stream":true,"block":0}

