data: {"seq":527,"ts":"2026-08-23T09:10:57.504Z","text":"sub_40D1FC analysis (UPnP HTTP request line parser):\n\n```\nv5 = *(a1+32) // request buffer\n// 1st token (method): copy up to 15 bytes into v7[16] — bounded ok\n// skip spaces; if starts with \"http://\", advance to next '/'\n// 2nd token (URL): copy up to 511 into v8[500]?? wait v8[7]!! \n```\n\nLook: `char v8[7];` — BUT `for (i=0; i<511 && *v5!=32 && *v5!=13; ++i) v8[i] = *v5++;` copies UP TO 511 bytes into v8 which is declared as 7 bytes! That's the classic **stack buffer overflow in upnphttp.c**: URL path copied into a 7-byte buffer v8 with bound 511.\n\nWait — let's double check stack layout: \n```\nchar v7[16];   // sp+40\nchar v8[7];    // sp+50\nchar v9[2];    // sp+57\nchar v10;      // sp+59\nchar v11[2];   // sp+5A\nchar v12[500]; // sp+5C\nvoid *src;     // sp+250\n_BYTE v14[2];  // sp+254\nconst char *v15; // sp+258\n```\nv8 at sp+50, 7 bytes → occupies sp+50..sp+56. Then v9 at sp+57, v10 at sp+59, v11 at sp+5A, v12[500] at sp+5C. So writing beyond 7 bytes into v8 would overwrite v9, v10, v11, then v12 (500 bytes)... So the overflow (up to 511 bytes from URL) would smash v12 (used later as buffer for SendResp_thumbnail/dlnafile) and beyond into saved registers. Hmm — although IDA might have mis-sized v8 (v8 is actually the tail of the URL buffer). Let's carefully recount: \n\n- i < 511 bound; copy into v8. If v8 were really 7 bytes, the \"511\" bound is bizarre — it suggests IDA mis-decoded a union: actually the URL copy loop `v8[i]` probably writes into a larger structure; the decompiler shows v8 as the start of a 512-byte area? But it listed v12[500] separately at sp+5C, right after v11[2] at sp+5A. So v8 (sp+50,7) + v9 (sp+57,2) + v10 (sp+59,1) + v11 (sp+5A,2) = sp+50..sp+5B (12 bytes), then v12 at sp+5C (500 bytes). So an over-7-byte copy into v8 smashes v9/v10/v11/v12.\n\nHmm wait, but that contradicts real minidlna source: upnphttp.c: \n```c\nchar * p; ...\nif (strncmp(req_buf, \"GET \", 4)==0) ...\nsprintf(HttpUrl, \"%s\", ...)?? \n```\nActually real minidlna (upnphttp.c GetHTTPResponse / Process_request): it copies the URL into a 512+ byte buffer? Let me recall real code:\n\n```c\nstatic void\nProcess_request(struct upnphttp * h, char * req_buf)\n{\n  ...\n  for (i = 0; i < 15 && req_buf[i] != ' ' && req_buf[i] != '\\r'; i++)\n    cmd[i] = req_buf[i];\n  cmd[i] = '\\0';\n  while (req_buf[i] == ' ') i++;\n  ...parse url...\n  for (i = 0; i < 512 && req_buf[i] != ' ' && req_buf[i] != '\\r'; i++)\n    url[i] = req_buf[i];\n```\nWait — in real minidlna upnphttp.c:\n\n```c\nchar cmd[16], url[512], ...\n```\nHmm. But the decompiled version has v8[7]?? Let me look at the actual size of the URL: the second loop bound is i<511 with v8 — total URLs up to 511 chars. v8[7]... that CANNOT be right; a 7-byte buffer copied with 511 bound would be a notorious bug that would've been caught. UNLESS the decompiler mislabeled: maybe v8 is the start of a region and v12[500] is part of the same global struct? sp+50 to sp+5C: v8 7 + v9 2 + v10 1 + v11 2 = 12 → sp+5C. So url copy starting at sp+50 with bound 511 writes from sp+50 to sp+50+511 = sp+20F — overflowing v8, v9, v10, v11, v12 (500), src (sp+250)... \n\nHmm wait, maybe v8..v12 are actually all part of ONE buffer in the original code and IDA split variable names: e.g. `char url[512]` at sp+50, and the \"v8[7]+v9[2]+v10[1]+v11[2]+v12[500]\" = 512 bytes total at sp+50..sp+250! 7+2+1+2+500 = 512. YES! v8 through v12 exactly tile sp+50..sp+250 (512 bytes). So the URL buffer is 512 bytes starting at sp+50 (\"url[512]\"), and IDA just split it into pieces because subsequent hander functions receive pointer to parts (v9 → SendResp_icon(a1, v9), v10 → SendResp_resizedimg(a1, &v10), v11 → SendResp_albumArt/caption(a1, v11), v12 → SendResp_thumbnail/dlnafile(a1, v12)).\n\nSo the URL copy bound 511 into 512-byte buffer is SAFE (copy at most 511 chars + NUL at v8[511]... wait bound: `for (i = 0; i < 511 && ...; ++i) v8[i] = *v5++;` then `v8[i] = 0` — so max 511 chars + NUL = 512 bytes. Exactly fits url[512]. Safe.\n\nOK so that's not a bug (classic IDA split-var artifact). The 3rd token (protocol \"HTTP/1.1\") copy bound 15 into a1+16 area (16 bytes) — safe.\n\nThe chunked/body decode loop with memmove(dest, src, *(a1+112)) — parsing \"Transfer-Encoding: chunked\"? This region (a1+116) & 0x100 flag... This decodes hex chunk lengths. dest starts at (a1+32)+(a1+44) and memmove of declared chunk length — bounded by request buffer validity? It's the \"read_request\" chunked decoding — minidlna upnphttp.c has a known issue? Not obviously.\n\nThis function is a router. Not the money shot.\n\nI've been going wide. Time to go DEEP on one thing with conviction: \n\n**The strongest dynamic tool in this session: stdin/file differential + fuzz. The most valuable real parser we can feed via file: minidlnad? No — config heavy. busybox? Old but real.**\n\nActually — wait. Let me reconsider **httpd** more seriously, since it's the #1 historically-vulnerable component of Archer C7 (many real CVEs: e.g., CVE-2019-16920 \"TP-Link Archer C7v2 httpd DoS\", CVE-2017-13772 \"stack overflow in TP-Link httpd\", command injection in cgi). And our net trace on :80 produced an EMPTY diff — but maybe the request DID get handled and baseline is ALSO handling the same functions (httpd main loop has identical coverage because qemu trace captures the accept loop regardless). Baseline for net seems to be \"connect and send nothing\"? If baseline sends nothing but our trigger sends GET /userRpm/..., then diff should show request parser functions... unless httpd under qemu failed early (no /tmp mount, no web dir) and closed the connection without reading.\n\nLet me test differently: since rcS shows httpd runs with no args, run httpd trace with request_path=\"/\" — should hit GoIndex/404 handlers...\n\nHmm, but I already spent one trace. Repeating similar probes risks another empty. Better: read httpd's main first to see what it needs (args, web root) — via fw_get_function_source on httpd main. Find httpd main addr: search? httpd has 4882 funcs. Use identification IN-014? main addr unknown. Use fw_search(\"main\") in job? pattern \"httpd\"? Let me fw_search pattern \"^main$\" limit... too broad. \n\nAlternative: the wmnet* functions (wmnetTcpRead etc.) are the accept/read layer; sub_420B48 reads/writes. The actual HTTP request parsing is likely httpMimeParse (0x444940) — \"HTTP mime parse\" (headers) — and httpLineUnescape. Let me read httpMimeParse full + httpLineUnescape + httpBase64decode","stream":true,"block":0}

