ential diff 45 confirmed real coverage incl. safe_read. A malformed script could crash ash/msh? BusyBox 1.01 ash is not known for easy crashes; unlikely.\n\nWhat about **libavformat/AVI parsing via minidlnad** — can't reach.\n\nHmm — what about running **minidlnad with argv to scan a directory (media rescan)**? minidlnad -R rescan; it would open files from a media dir... but the media dir is config (from /etc/minidlna.conf or defaults \"Video/Pictures/Music\" dirs relative to cwd?). Actually minidlna default media dir? Empty → no scanning. And scanning requires sqlite.\n\nRegroup: What about **UCLITED** (usr/bin/uclited, d61e67ed)? It communicates with the router daemon over a socket. TP-Link uclited - \"uCli\" (universal CLI) daemon — parses CLI commands from a TCP/unix socket (local management). identification IN-011: tcp, port null. uclited parses command lines → cmdexec sinks? TP-Link's uclited historically has command injection via its management channel (local). The attack surface might have uclited paths. It's a small binary (d61e67ed20163b7fee8bd3c606fb0331). Let me probe its surface & main: fw_get_surface AS-011 + main search.\n\nActually, let me reconsider **ipcserver** (usr/sbin/ipcserver, 0b3a37ef) — TP-Link IPC daemon — parses IPC commands; known TP-Link ipcserver had buffer overflow (CVE-2016-1554? \"TP-Link ipcserver stack overflow\" — YES! There's a known TP-Link **ipcserver** vulnerability: CVE-2014-???? hmm. Actually I recall \"TP-Link Archer C7 ipcserver command injection / buffer overflow\" from a well-known advisory","stream":true,"block":0}

event: thinking
data: {"seq":556,"ts":"2026-08-23T09:11:07.415Z","text":"httpMimeParse: header-name/header-value dispatch via registered functions (httpGenList*). Safe pattern.\nhttpLineUnescape: in-place unescape, bounded by strlen, no overflow.\nhttpBase64decode: writes into a2 with check `if (v6 + 2 - v20++ < v4)` bounded by a3. Safe-ish.\n\nhttpd's normalizers are safe. I'm burning budget on reading safe code.\n\n**Decision: pivot to the approach with the highest probability of a defensible finding: differential fuzzing of the media file parsers that are genuinely complex, ancient, and known-buggy, via the FILE path — using minidlnad's thumbnail/resized image handlers which read files directly.**\n\nWait — SendResp_thumbnail (0x41065c) / SendResp_resizedimg (0x410ae8) / SendResp_albumArt (0x40fe74) — the carrier bindings show these functions read files (fread at 0x449b64 ×3, read at 0x42412c). These are reached via HTTP path /Thumbnails/<id> etc. The ID refers to a DB row; the file path is looked up in sqlite DB. Without DB, these handlers 404. Not drivable.\n\n**Alternative pragmatic winner: `fw_request_fuzz` whole-binary on minidlnad?** No.\n\nOK, let me think about what traces the 432 existing runs covered — probably previous sessions already fuzzed/traced many parser functions. The with_diff list only shows a432876827e2 — the ONLY non-empty diff ever. The 293 ok_empty_diff and 55 ok_empty_diff_trigger_error. The extensive empty diffs suggest previous runs were mostly probes like ours.\n\nGiven all constraints, the honest, highest-value action now: **find and verify one concrete memory-safety bug through focused static + a crash PoC via fw_qemu_exec (which we know works), recorded as a finding with reachability=static-only + PoC + crash evidence.**\n\nThe crash-checkable targets: the **media parsing libraries** can be driven through qemu_exec only by running minidlnad — no. BUT there IS a classic direct-file-primitive available: **busybox `msh`/`sh` script parsing** — differential diff 45 confirmed real coverage incl. safe_read. A malformed script could crash ash/msh? BusyBox 1.01 ash is not known for easy crashes; unlikely.\n\nWhat about **libavformat/AVI parsing via minidlnad** — can't reach.\n\nHmm — what about running **minidlnad with argv to scan a directory (media rescan)**? minidlnad -R rescan; it would open files from a media dir... but the media dir is config (from /etc/minidlna.conf or defaults \"Video/Pictures/Music\" dirs relative to cwd?). Actually minidlna default media dir? Empty → no scanning. And scanning requires sqlite.\n\nRegroup: What about **UCLITED** (usr/bin/uclited, d61e67ed)? It communicates with the router daemon over a socket. TP-Link uclited - \"uCli\" (universal CLI) daemon — parses CLI commands from a TCP/unix socket (local management). identification IN-011: tcp, port null. uclited parses command lines → cmdexec sinks? TP-Link's uclited historically has command injection via its management channel (local). The attack surface might have uclited paths. It's a small binary (d61e67ed20163b7fee8bd3c606fb0331). Let me probe its surface & main: fw_get_surface AS-011 + main search.\n\nActually, let me reconsider **ipcserver** (usr/sbin/ipcserver, 0b3a37ef) — TP-Link IPC daemon — parses IPC commands; known TP-Link ipcserver had buffer overflow (CVE-2016-1554? \"TP-Link ipcserver stack overflow\" — YES! There's a known TP-Link **ipcserver** vulnerability: CVE-2014-???? hmm. Actually I recall \"TP-Link Archer C7 ipcserver command injection / buffer overflow\" from a well-known advisory: \"Embedi - TP-Link multiple vulnerabilities\" (2018): ipcserver heap overflow? Let me just look at AS-007 (ipcserver) + its main/dispatcher.\n\nLet me pull AS-007 and AS-011 surfaces and the mains of ipcserver/uclited in parallel. Main addresses unknown yet; surfaces may give routing/dispatcher names.","stream":true,"block":0}

