data: {"seq":600,"ts":"2026-08-23T09:11:23.765Z","text":"The `^main$` search returned 43 results, mostly different binaries. I saw ipcserver entries? Not yet — I need to scroll. The ipcserver binary md5=0b3a37ef... qualified names use binary name prefix: I see athstats, radartool, apstats, iwconfig, tc, arpping, xl2tpd, pppd, athdiag, uclited. No ipcserver main in the first 10 — limit 100 but the response got truncated? It returned 43 total; I see maybe 12. The ipcserver main may be there. Let me instead directly probe ipcserver functions: I have sub_400750 and sub_400EB0 (init/atexit). The main for ipcserver (36 funcs) is probably 0x400D00-ish. Alternatively use fw_get_function_source brief on ipcserver addresses 0x400xxx low... We can use the AS: \"accept/read in main\". The binary is small; functions probably 0x400600..0x400F00.\n\nActually — wait. sub_400750/sub_400EB0 are __libc_csu analogous (init arrays) which appear in EVERY binary (dhcp6s had identical 0x424080/0x402660, minidlnad...). So ipcserver's dispatcher \"sub_400EB0/sub_400750\" in the surface are ALSO init functions — surface's dispatch stage is again garbage. The identification mislabeled.\n\nTime management: I've done deep static hunting across many binaries without a confirmed bug. Let me now **commit to a dynamic-first strategy**: pick the best TCP-reachable protocol parsers and drive them with net traces to find REAL parsing diffs; then read ONLY the diff functions to find the bug; then fuzz.\n\nCandidate TCP parsers with known juicy protocols:\n1. **dhcp6s ctl channel** — TCP. Port? Need off_43C074 default. Read dhcp6_ctl_init? Or just search the binary for the port constant. Actually KAME dhcp6s: DHCP6CTL_PORT = 5555? Let me check dhcp6_ctl_init source quickly — it reads off_43C074 (from -p). Default value off_43C074... In main, 'p' case: `off_43C074 = optarg`. Default = 0 → dhcp6_ctl_init(off_43C070, off_43C074, 5, ...) with 0 → uses default port constant inside. Read dhcp6_ctl_init.\n\nActually which contains the port: dhcp6_ctl_init(addr, port, family, sock). With port=0, it uses DHCP6CTL_PORT. KAME's dhcp6ctl.h: #define DHCP6CTL_PORT 0x0691?? hmm. I remember \"dhcp6ctl\" port 5555 in some implementations; KAME uses \"695\" hmm. Let me read dhcp6_ctl_init to get the constant. If ctl port is a fixed number, then net trace on that TCP port with a control command could differential-test — REAL pre-auth? Control channel uses auth (dhcp6_ctl_authinit from","stream":true,"block":0}

