ually which contains the port: dhcp6_ctl_init(addr, port, family, sock). With port=0, it uses DHCP6CTL_PORT. KAME's dhcp6ctl.h: #define DHCP6CTL_PORT 0x0691?? hmm. I remember \"dhcp6ctl\" port 5555 in some implementations; KAME uses \"695\" hmm. Let me read dhcp6_ctl_init to get the constant. If ctl port is a fixed number, then net trace on that TCP port with a control command could differential-test — REAL pre-auth? Control channel uses auth (dhcp6_ctl_authinit from /etc/dhcp6sctlkey). With key present in firmware image, an attacker cannot read it (it's in flash rootfs... actually rootfs is read-only but this key ships in the image — Me: any attacker with LAN access can't read flash, so auth protects). But KAME ctl auth = HMAC-MD5 with key → commands authenticated. Not exploitable pre-auth unless key predictable/weak. Drop.\n\n2. **vsftpd :21** — stock; the FTP \"line reader\" ftp_getline. vsftpd 2.0.x... has CVE-2011-0762? (a2d? no) — \"vsftpd 2.3.2-2.3.4 backdoor\" was specific. vsftpd 2.3.4 backdoor triggered by \":)\". If this TP-Link vsftpd is 2.3.4, that's a well-known (but patched) backdoor. Let me check its version string via fw_qemu_exec (run vsftpd with no args prints version? vsftpd with no args prints usage/version to stderr?). Actually running vsftpd prints nothing; but strings... can't grep.\n\nHmm.\n\n3. **httpd :80** — already empty diff. Maybe httpd didn't even accept because under qemu-user it daemonizes (daemon() double-fork) and the trace harness connection attempt happens while... The trace harness presumably connects after process starts; daemon() in qemu-user: fork works. But httpd might bind only after reading config from flash (not mounted). rcS starts httpd with no args — it self-configures from nvram-ish /tmp files? Whatever.\n\nHonestly, at this point, the most defensible *find* would be from a parser we can actually drive with the file","stream":true,"block":0}

