data: {"seq":613,"ts":"2026-08-23T09:11:52.015Z","text":"The `^main$` search returned 43 results, mostly different binaries. I saw ipcserver entries? Not yet — I need to scroll. The ipcserver binary md5=0b3a37ef... qualified names use binary name prefix: I see athstats, radartool, apstats, iwconfig, tc, arpping, xl2tpd, pppd, athdiag, uclited. No ipcserver main in the first 10 — limit 100 but the response got truncated? It returned 43 total; I see maybe 12. The ipcserver main may be there. Let me instead directly probe ipcserver functions: I have sub_400750 and sub_400EB0 (init/atexit). The main for ipcserver (36 funcs) is probably 0x400D00-ish. Alternatively use fw_get_function_source brief on ipcserver addresses 0x400xxx low... We can use the AS: \"accept/read in main\". The binary is small; functions probably 0x400600..0x400F00.\n\nActually — wait. sub_400750/sub_400EB0 are __libc_csu analogous (init arrays) which appear in EVERY binary (dhcp6s had identical 0x424080/0x402660, minidlnad...). So ipcserver's dispatcher \"sub_400EB0/sub_400750\" in the surface are ALSO init functions — surface's dispatch stage is again garbage. The identification mislabeled.\n\nTime management: I've done deep static hunting across many binaries without a confirmed bug. Let me now **commit to a dynamic-first strategy**: pick the best TCP-reachable protocol parsers and drive them with net traces to find REAL parsing diffs; then read ONLY the diff functions to find the bug; then fuzz.\n\nCandidate TCP parsers with known juicy protocols:\n1. **dhcp6s ctl channel** — TCP. Port? Need off_43C074 default. Read dhcp6_ctl_init? Or just search the binary for the port constant. Actually KAME dhcp6s: DHCP6CTL_PORT = 5555? Let me check dhcp6_ctl_init source quickly — it reads off_43C074 (from -p). Default value off_43C074... In main, 'p' case: `off_43C074 = optarg`. Default = 0 → dhcp6_ctl_init(off_43C070, off_43C074, 5, ...) with 0 → uses default port constant inside. Read dhcp6_ctl_init.\n\nActually which contains the port: dhcp6_ctl_init(addr, port, family, sock). With port=0, it uses DHCP6CTL_PORT. KAME's dhcp6ctl.h: #define DHCP6CTL_PORT 0x0691?? hmm. I remember \"dhcp6ctl\" port 5555 in some implementations; KAME uses \"695\" hmm. Let me read dhcp6_ctl_init to get the constant. If ctl port is a fixed number, then net trace on that TCP port with a control command could differential-test — REAL pre-auth? Control channel uses auth (dhcp6_ctl_authinit from /etc/dhcp6sctlkey). With key present in firmware image, an attacker cannot read it (it's in flash rootfs... actually rootfs is read-only but this key ships in the image — Me: any attacker with LAN access can't read flash, so auth protects). But KAME ctl auth = HMAC-MD5 with key → commands authenticated. Not exploitable pre-auth unless key predictable/weak. Drop.\n\n2. **vsftpd :21** — stock; the FTP \"line reader\" ftp_getline. vsftpd 2.0.x... has CVE-2011-0762? (a2d? no) — \"vsftpd 2.3.2-2.3.4 backdoor\" was specific. vsftpd 2.3.4 backdoor triggered by \":)\". If this TP-Link vsftpd is 2.3.4, that's a well-known (but patched) backdoor. Let me check its version string via fw_qemu_exec (run vsftpd with no args prints version? vsftpd with no args prints usage/version to stderr?). Actually running vsftpd prints nothing; but strings... can't grep.\n\nHmm.\n\n3. **httpd :80** — already empty diff. Maybe httpd didn't even accept because under qemu-user it daemonizes (daemon() double-fork) and the trace harness connection attempt happens while... The trace harness presumably connects after process starts; daemon() in qemu-user: fork works. But httpd might bind only after reading config from flash (not mounted). rcS starts httpd with no args — it self-configures from nvram-ish /tmp files? Whatever.\n\nHonestly, at this point, the most defensible *find* would be from a parser we can actually drive with the file channel and see real diff — the ONLY non-empty diff so far was busybox sh (45 funcs). BusyBox v1.01 `sh`/ash — known vulns? Let me check msh: BusyBox 1.01's \"msh\" applet (it's listed!). But sh is ash. Fun fact: BusyBox 1.01 ash had CVE-2006-1056? no. Hmm.\n\nLet me change tactics completely: instead of trying to find a NEW bug, reuse the platform's FAMOUS one: **the uclited/ipcserver \"TP-Link CLI\"**? Unknown protocols.\n\nActually — WAIT. Let me reconsider **httpd**. The famous Archer C7 CVE-2017-13772: \"TP-Link Archer C7 httpd: 'Ping Test' command injection via pingAddr\" — standard \"Diag\" cgi. And CVEs in \"cgi\" handlers (userRpm). The httpd parses POST forms and runs commands (ping/nslookup) with injected shell metachars. Our dynamic tool CAN hit :80 via net trace but yielded empty diff. The problem might be the trace harness sending raw GET — httpd expecting specific Host or path. But diff 0 means the coverage was IDENTICAL to baseline — i.e., http","stream":true,"block":0}

