h`/ash — known vulns? Let me check msh: BusyBox 1.01's \"msh\" applet (it's listed!). But sh is ash. Fun fact: BusyBox 1.01 ash had CVE-2006-1056? no. Hmm.\n\nLet me change tactics completely: instead of trying to find a NEW bug, reuse the platform's FAMOUS one: **the uclited/ipcserver \"TP-Link CLI\"**? Unknown protocols.\n\nActually — WAIT. Let me reconsider **httpd**. The famous Archer C7 CVE-2017-13772: \"TP-Link Archer C7 httpd: 'Ping Test' command injection via pingAddr\" — standard \"Diag\" cgi. And CVEs in \"cgi\" handlers (userRpm). The httpd parses POST forms and runs commands (ping/nslookup) with injected shell metachars. Our dynamic tool CAN hit :80 via net trace but yielded empty diff. The problem might be the trace harness sending raw GET — httpd expecting specific Host or path. But diff 0 means the coverage was IDENTICAL to baseline — i.e., httpd likely FAILED to start under qemu (e.g., cannot access /dev/nvram or /tmp files, exits) so both baseline & trigger are the same short run. Let me verify with fw_qemu_exec: run httpd with no argv for 5 seconds and see output/status. If it says error (missing file), we know why.\n\nActually faster: run fw_qemu_exec httpd with argv empty, seconds=5. Observe rc. Then decide.\n\nAlso let me reconsider target **dnsproxy** — it parses DNS on UDP/TCP? identification IN-005 says protocol udp. UDP — not net-traceable. Skip.\n\n**bpalogin** (UDP) — skip.\n\nOK here's my final focused plan (budget-aware):\n1. fw_qemu_exec httpd: check whether it can even start under qemu (why net trace was empty). \n2. If httpd starts and waits","stream":true,"block":0}

