data: {"seq":654,"ts":"2026-08-23T09:12:57.626Z","text":"IMPORTANT: running httpd with no argv under qemu-user → **segfault (signal 11)** — an early crash — then timeout (watchdog killed it at 9.68s? Actually status timeout, signal 9 — watchdog; output tail shows SIGSEGV). So httpd crashes on startup under qemu-user (probably due to missing /proc or config). That explains the empty net diffs: httpd segfaults before/without handling requests, both baseline and trigger identical.\n\nThe segfault in httpd startup: not our bug (environmental — missing nvram access etc.).\n\nSo httpd dynamic verification via qemu is a dead end for now (environmental crash, can't reach handlers). Same likely applies to other daemons that need /proc//dev/nvram.\n\nGiven qemu-user limits, our reliable dynamic windows:\n1. busybox applets (worked: sh, md5sum) — scripts/unknowns.\n2. Programs that read stdin/file and don't need /proc: e.g., `test`, `cat`, `logger`, `echo`... trivial.\n\nLet me reconsider the ONE striking result so far: busybox `sh` reading the file \"echo hello\" gave 45 diff functions. BusyBox v1.01 — the `msh`/`sh` (ash) shell parser. BusyBox 1.01 ash has a documented heap-based buffer overflow? Searching memory: BusyBox 1.01 CVE list: CVE-2006-1056 (wget), CVE-2006-1057 (httpd chunked)?, CVE-2007-5199 (?), CVE-2011-5325... none ash-specific until CVE-2019-5747? etc.\n\nRather than busybox shells, **what about `tftp` client** (busybox applet) — needs server.\n\nOK. Let me step WAY back and think about what this session can actually deliver as a CONFIRMED finding:\n\nGiven: \n- net traces on daemons fail (env crashes; httpd segfault).\n- stdin/file traces work ONLY for programs that naturally read them: busybox applets (sh/msh/md5sum/cat/...).\n- qemu_exec crash-check works.\n- fuzz (AFL qemu whole-binary) probably works on stdin-reading programs too.\n\n=> The realistic deliverable: a bug in a busybox applet that reads stdin/files, discovered via fuzz + crash, e.g.:\n- `msh`/`sh` script parser (complex, 45+ diff functions — but shell parsers rarely crash on malformed input).\n- `logger`? trivial.\n- `top`? reads /proc.\n- `tftp`, `udhcpc`/`udhcpd` — network.\n- `ping` — argv.\n\nAlternatively — **re-examine the earlier trace a432876827e2's diff functions for a shell-parse bug**? Unlikely.\n\nHmm, wait — what about **`tar`/decompression**? Not in the applet list (no tar/gunzip/unzip/cpio/gzip applets in busybox, per the usage list: arping, brctl, cat, chmod, date, df, echo, ethreg, false, getty, ifconfig, init, insmod, kill, klogd, linuxrc, ln, logger, login, logread, ls, lsmod, mount, msh, ping, ps, reboot, rm, rmmod, route, sh, sleep, syslogd, test, tftp, top, true, udhcpc, udhcpd, umount, vconfig).\n\n**arping** — reads argv + sends packets. `brctl` — argv. Ifconfig — argv... All argv-based, or network.\n\nSo busybox itself offers little for stdin-file finding.\n\n**Let me reconsider dropbear (not decompiled)**: dynamic trace via :22 SSH — dropbear under qemu should RUN (it doesn't need /proc heavily; it needs host keys from /etc/dropbear — if missing, it generates? dropbear generates keys on first run writing to /etc/dropbear — read-only rootfs would fail... under qemu-user rootfs might be writable? The sandbox rootfs is a docker dir — writable. dropbear could generate host keys and listen!). A net trace on :22 with a raw SSH banner could show real KEX handling diff! Dropbear version? It's not decompiled, so no source — but we can still detect *coverage diff* and then fuzz the KEX parser for crashes via net...\n\nHold on, fw_request_trace via=net: \"sends HTTP GET or payload/payloads_hex on the port\". For SSH we'd send payloads_hex (multi-packet conversation): banner exchange: client sends \"SSH-2.0-...\" then KEXINIT etc. If dropbear's KEXINIT parser (buf_getstring etc.) has a real overflow and we fuzz... but fuzz tool (AFL qemu) can't do sockets for dropbear either.\n\nRealistically: **net trace only provides coverage diff observation; it can crash-check via the harness?** No — fw_get_trace trigger_result won't crash a daemon typically (it sends one request). fw_qemu_exec could run dropbear and... it needs a client.\n\nOK. Let me accept: this session's successful dynamic surface is stdin/file. The most promising REAL bug I've seen statically that is ALSO dynamically driveable via file:\n\n**minidlnad media parsing** — but minidlnad won't run (needs config/db, and it's a daemon binding ports — actually if it CAN run under qemu: it binds 8200 & 1900; and scans media dirs. If config absent → default options? sub_405D5C reads options file /etc/minidlna.conf; if missing → defaults? It might default to no media dirs and just serve UPnP XML (functional but no media). Then HTTP traces to :8200 would work → /rootDesc.xml, /MediaItems/... but no DB → empty lists. The thumbnail handlers need DB rows → 404. So no file parsing reachable without media db. BUT we could run minidlnad and inject a sqlite DB? Too much.\n\n**Decision**: pivot to fuzzing a busybox msh/sh? Weak.\n\nActually, let me revisit identification","stream":true,"block":0}

