ad buffer), memmove copies beyond the buffer — but memmove's destination/source are inside the SAME buffer (in-place decode) — since src is also within the buffer and dest≤src region... Actually dest starts after headers and src starts after dest; each chunk is copied forward in place — the total copied is sum of chunk sizes; if sum exceeds buffer capacity, dest goes past buffer end → heap/stack overflow of the upnphttp structure's buffer (which is a fixed 2048/8192 byte in-struct buffer). This chunked decode is in REAL minidlna upnphttp.c? Real minidlna doesn't support chunked... this looks TP-Link-modified (\"(a1+116)&0x100\" flag). Reading the request happens in wmnet/upnphttp read loop with a fixed buffer; the chunked section flag... \n\nThe overflow depends on whether a1 (upnphttp struct) buffer is fixed-size and whether the req buffer is large enough. This is worth checking: who calls sub_40D1FC and how big is the request buffer? The request buffer at a1+32, and total request length at a1+36/40. In minidlna, the upnphttp struct has `char * req_buf` (malloc'd?) — hmm, in minidlna, struct upnphttp { char req_buf[HTTPREADBUFFER_SIZE]; ... } with 2048. Actually:\n\n```c\nstruct upnphttp {\n  int socket;\n  time_t req_time;\n  char *","stream":true,"block":0}

