data: {"seq":735,"ts":"2026-08-23T09:13:28.918Z","text":"Process_upnphttp: buffer is realloc'd to hold everything; sub_40C538 finds \"\\r\\n\\r\\n\" end of headers → a1+44 = header-end offset, a1+40 = content-length (from header). Then sub_40D1FC runs when enough data arrived (a1+36 - a1+44 >= a1+40), or immediately after first recv with the full thing (state0 path).\n\nsub_40D1FC chunked decode:\n- (a1+116)&0x100 → chunked. Then:\n```\nv3 = (a1+32) + (a1+44)  // start of body\ndest = v3\nloop:\n  (a1+112) = strtol(nptr, &src, 16)  // nptr starts at body start\n  if (!chunklen || src==nptr) break\n  skip to CRLF; src = src+2\n  memmove(dest, src, chunklen)\n  dest += chunklen\n  nptr = src + chunklen   // next chunk header candidate\n(a1+40) = dest - v3\n(a1+36) = dest - (a1+32)\n```\nThe memmove copies from src onward WITHIN the original buffer — since dest ≤ src always (dest starts at body, src starts after first chunk header), this is in-place left-shift. dest advances by chunklen each time. The TOTAL copied = sum of chunklen ≤ declared chunk sizes. If the attacker declares a huge chunklen (strtol 16 hex → up to ~2^31), memmove(dest, src, 0x7FFFFFFF) — wow, memmove with gigantic length would read src which lies inside the realloc'd buffer of size (a1+36)... memmove copies chunklen bytes from src (within buffer) to dest (within buffer) — if chunklen exceeds remaining buffer, it reads beyond src AND writes beyond dest → huge overflow. BUT wait: is there a constraint that a1+36 (received length) limits? The loop source pointer advances: after first iteration nptr = src + chunklen... for the memmove, the actual bytes copied are from src; the count is arbitrary chunklen. If the buffer has ONLY received N bytes but chunklen = 0x100000, memmove reads/writes 1MB → heap buffer overflow (writes beyond realloc'd buffer → overwrites heap metadata / adjacent chunks).\n\nIn the real code path: state 1 (a1+12==1)? Let's trace: sub_40D1FC is called from Process_upnphttp in state0 path (after first recv + header parse) and in state2. The state machine: New_upnphttp sets state 0; after first recv & header parse → sub_40D1FC sets a1+12=100 (response) or 2 (for POST body?); \"if ((a1+116)&0x100)\" → chunked → parse...\n\nBut actually — hold on. In sub_40D1FC the initial condition was:\n```\nif ((*(a1+116) & 0x100) != 0) {\n  if (*(a1+112)) { *(a1+12)=2; return; }   // a1+112!=0 means previous chunk parse left size -> read more\n  v3 = (a1+32)+(a1+44); ...\n```\nWait no — looking at the decompilation again, the chunked decode in sub_40D1FC happens under state... Hmm, actually the sub_40D1FC code I read had the chunked decode only when (a1+116)&0x100 — same as sub_40A714's chunked pre-scan which SETS a1+112 = -1 or the last chunk size. Look at sub_40A714 end:\n```\nif ((a1+116)&0x100) == 0 goto LABEL_183;\n*(a1+112) = -1;\nif ((a1+44) >= (a1+36)) return;   // not all data received yet\nwalk chunks: (a1+112)=strtol(s,...); s += size+2 ...\nif (v37==s) -> a1+112=-1\n```\nHmm — sub_40A714 *pre-scans* the chunked body: it walks chunk sizes to the end; each strtol is bounded by the received buffer (s walks within a1+32..a1+32+a1+36). If a chunk size exceeds remaining buffer, s could skip beyond a1+36 — the loop condition `(unsigned int)s < (a1+32)+(a1+36)` bounds the walk. So pre-scan just validates.\n\nThen decode in sub_40D1FC: memmove sizes come from parsed chunk headers within the buffer; a \"strtol of 0x100000\" with only e.g. 100 bytes received → memmove(dest, src, 0x100000) — dest advances by 1MB — writes 1MB past the small buffer?? BUT: realloc in the state0 path grew buffer to (a1+36)+1 (received size). And in the chunked path, sub_40D1FC is re-invoked via state 2 when (a1+36)-(a1+44) >= (a1+40)... hmm the interplay is complex.\n\nBUT WAIT: memmove with src+len beyond buffer — memmove reads beyond src too. Actually here: after first chunk \"10\\r\\n\" + 16 bytes + \"\\r\\n\" then \"1000000\\r\\n...\" — strtol reads size 0x1000000; skip to next CRLF (found); src+=2; memmove(dest, src, 0x1000000): reads 16MB from a small buffer → heap overread + overflow WRITE 16MB at dest (which is at body start + ...). With a modestly-sized receive buffer (~2048), that's a huge heap overflow. BUT the pre-scan in sub_40A714 (end) walks the same chunk sizes with bounds: `if (v37==s) { a1+112=-1 }` and sets a1+112 accordingly...\n\nHmm wait — which one runs first? Order: Process_upnphttp state0: recv → append → sub_40C538 (find header end) → sets a1+44, a1+40 → sub_40D1FC(a1). In sub_40D1FC, the chunked block requires (a1+116)&0x100 (set by header parse when Transfer-Encoding: chunked). But sub_40A714 (header parse + chunk pre-scan) is only called in state1 (a1+12==1): `if (a1+12==1){ sub_40A714(a1); sub_40C8B8(a1); }`. Who sets state=1? Presumably after first parse discovers POST content-length... In state0 path, when headers parsed and body pending (a1+40>0), maybe sub_40C8B8? Hmm, not visible. This state machine needs more reading (sub_40C8B8, sub_40C538).\n\nRather than fully tracing, the RISK: chunked decode memmove with attacker-controlled count into realloc'd buffer without verifying count ≤ remaining bytes. This is a genuine candidate heap overflow (CWE-122). In upstream minidlna, \"Process_request\" — the","stream":true,"block":0}

