data: {"seq":745,"ts":"2026-08-23T09:13:32.822Z","text":"Process_upnphttp: buffer is realloc'd to hold everything; sub_40C538 finds \"\\r\\n\\r\\n\" end of headers → a1+44 = header-end offset, a1+40 = content-length (from header). Then sub_40D1FC runs when enough data arrived (a1+36 - a1+44 >= a1+40), or immediately after first recv with the full thing (state0 path).\n\nsub_40D1FC chunked decode:\n- (a1+116)&0x100 → chunked. Then:\n```\nv3 = (a1+32) + (a1+44)  // start of body\ndest = v3\nloop:\n  (a1+112) = strtol(nptr, &src, 16)  // nptr starts at body start\n  if (!chunklen || src==nptr) break\n  skip to CRLF; src = src+2\n  memmove(dest, src, chunklen)\n  dest += chunklen\n  nptr = src + chunklen   // next chunk header candidate\n(a1+40) = dest - v3\n(a1+36) = dest - (a1+32)\n```\nThe memmove copies from src onward WITHIN the original buffer — since dest ≤ src always (dest starts at body, src starts after first chunk header), this is in-place left-shift. dest advances by chunklen each time. The TOTAL copied = sum of chunklen ≤ declared chunk sizes. If the attacker declares a huge chunklen (strtol 16 hex → up to ~2^31), memmove(dest, src, 0x7FFFFFFF) — wow, memmove with gigantic length would read src which lies inside the realloc'd buffer of size (a1+36)... memmove copies chunklen bytes from src (within buffer) to dest (within buffer) — if chunklen exceeds remaining buffer, it reads beyond src AND writes beyond dest → huge overflow. BUT wait: is there a constraint that a1+36 (received length) limits? The loop source pointer advances: after first iteration nptr = src + chunklen... for the memmove, the actual bytes copied are from src; the count is arbitrary chunklen. If the buffer has ONLY received N bytes but chunklen = 0x100000, memmove reads/writes 1MB → heap buffer overflow (writes beyond realloc'd buffer → overwrites heap metadata / adjacent chunks).\n\nIn the real code path: state 1 (a1+12==1)? Let's trace: sub_40D1FC is called from Process_upnphttp in state