xt CRLF (found); src+=2; memmove(dest, src, 0x1000000): reads 16MB from a small buffer → heap overread + overflow WRITE 16MB at dest (which is at body start + ...). With a modestly-sized receive buffer (~2048), that's a huge heap overflow. BUT the pre-scan in sub_40A714 (end) walks the same chunk sizes with bounds: `if (v37==s) { a1+112=-1 }` and sets a1+112 accordingly...\n\nHmm wait — which one runs first? Order: Process_upnphttp state0: recv → append → sub_40C538 (find header end) → sets a1+44, a1+40 → sub_40D1FC(a1). In sub_40D1FC, the chunked block requires (a1+116)&0x100 (set by header parse when Transfer-Encoding: chunked). But sub_40A714 (header parse + chunk pre-scan) is only called in state1 (a1+12==1): `if (a1+12==1){ sub_40A714(a1); sub_40C8B8(a1); }`. Who sets state=1? Presumably after first parse discovers POST content-length... In state0 path, when headers parsed and body pending (a1+40>0), maybe sub_40C8B8? Hmm, not visible. This state machine needs more reading (sub_40C8B8, sub_40C538).\n\nRather than fully tracing, the RISK: chunked decode memmove with attacker-controlled count into realloc'd buffer without verifying count ≤ remaining bytes. This is a genuine candidate heap overflow (CWE-122). In upstream minidlna, \"Process_request\" — the chunked body aggregation... hmm, upstream minidlna has this exact pattern? minidlna's upnphttp.c (recent) : \n\n```c\nelse if(h->req_chunksize) { /* chunked */ ... }\n```\nHmm, not sure TP-Link added Transfer-Encoding: chunked handling — the stock minidlna does NOT parse chunked bodies; this looks TP-Link-customized.\n\nGiven time, let me VERIFY the overflow with fw_qemu_exec on the real thing? minidlnad needs full runtime... not feasible for a network POST. \n\nAlternatively — **fuzz the function via fw_request_fuzz function-targeted?** MIPS, function mode discouraged.\n\nActually, hmm, can we drive minidlnad's upnphttp parser with fw_qemu_exec using argv to make minidlnad read a file as request? No.\n\nLet me pause: is there a *file*/stdin-based way to reach a parser with similar daemon? Not for this.\n\nDECISION: I've hit the session's practical limit for *dynamically verified* new bugs within reason. The strongest verifiable static candidate with a plausible dynamic path remains this chunked-body memmove. But reachability=static-only, confidence low-mid, and record_finding rules want real. An \"static-only\" finding capped at 0.7 confidence is allowed — with careful call_chain and honest severity (medium at most, memory corruption without checksec static evidence ≤0.6 per rule!). Actually minidlnad checksec? Not fetched. The rule says \"memory-corruption without checksec evidence ≤0.6\". And reachability static-only caps 0.7.\n\nI need to consider whether the chunked memmove is really triggerable: the memmove count = chunklen from hex parse, unbounded by remaining buffer. For it to be reachable, need HTTP request with Transfer-Encoding: chunked + huge chunk length, and the state machine to reach sub_40D1FC's chunked block. Given the code: any POST/GET with \"Transfer-Encoding: chunked\" sets flag 0x100 in header parse (sub_40A714 sets 0x100 when value==\"chunked\"). but the decode path runs","stream":true,"block":0}

